#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253363 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096702 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75fd35db-6b4e-5820-b6ac-925d24a0f117" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "133fede6-e6b3-5934-9ab6-600b897e76c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250719} {:answer/id #uuid "7bb9ca10-8ea3-5cda-814e-af155fd838bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251583} {:answer/id #uuid "c8c06d53-55cc-51d9-bc54-73807258d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252328} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c2b286-9473-5923-a0df-50419f5281e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252465} {:answer/id #uuid "ef673879-992e-531a-9cc6-362a14d4f889" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252648}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7bb9ca10-8ea3-5cda-814e-af155fd838bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251583} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f1d9cb5-e489-5d90-befc-482609c96a6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253053}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b52b18c-f18d-51d2-9931-b8f3ebe75944" :question/math-academy-id "q-11756" :question/problem "![](images/58/58c92cc542f027ec882191d2b4631f9fcf3704820b2825a18f30dbdc3f59d07b.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-4,2).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-4,2),$ we can substitute $x=-4$ and $y=2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {2}^{2} & =4p(-4) \\\\ 4 & =-16p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{1}{4}$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129121 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f8fa2b3-ccaa-5390-89fa-03e338cf8690" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Amplitude of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42d9acc7-d7bc-5ee0-a5fe-5e4dd49f1b24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251112} {:answer/id #uuid "62a72008-6a6e-556c-809d-015ca2b2740f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251358} {:answer/id #uuid "d1881db5-b1bc-59b9-9956-32e11311533c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252402} {:answer/id #uuid "f784db78-b2a6-5828-92f9-43c51bb6371d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252716} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfe367d-16eb-5c43-87a8-b57680a82f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252780}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f784db78-b2a6-5828-92f9-43c51bb6371d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252716} :answer-field/id #uuid "e24beebb-313f-5175-ae17-e942cb6940f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253192}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1489edd6-f3f4-5ab1-9410-746b5fd6547f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117570" :question/problem "What is the amplitude of the function $f(x)=4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+3)?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $f(x)=A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the amplitude is $|A|.$\n\nSince $A=4,$ we have that the amplitude is $|A|=|4|=4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253369 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129122 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1eeb42af-74ee-53d7-af9f-be171f77968f" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Period of a Transformed Sinusoidal Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4161544d-5503-509c-a919-47b75546d693" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251094} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0b8f2d-8bf9-5548-96dd-fe899f5d298f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252036} {:answer/id #uuid "ba23df8f-a679-5d5c-bde8-2b7238d24224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252191} {:answer/id #uuid "c9834705-9dcd-5c24-afb6-f095959807d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252335} {:answer/id #uuid "f25fe0b5-3a2d-5c1e-b5a3-425fbf7a30c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252674}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c9834705-9dcd-5c24-afb6-f095959807d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252335} :answer-field/id #uuid "918ffa31-2155-56a2-9928-59d9d880148e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "53685d44-d788-5019-bc63-97c441bd2453" :question/math-academy-id "q-117578" :question/problem "What is the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})?$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function in the form $y=A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{3}{4}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{3}{4})}=\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203961 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58c7cd44-30a9-5f61-822c-13640ad41c93" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Zeros of a Transformed Sine or Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d740ebb-367e-5b13-abb3-4aaa7aaffcc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251327} {:answer/id #uuid "76a2175d-f7a4-5539-8d5c-3821bd16c125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251531} {:answer/id #uuid "a02621c3-5f65-5847-b218-aabe23216fce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251935} {:answer/id #uuid "cce190ce-0319-5382-a9e1-b39ca5b6c857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252359} {:answer/id #uuid "e26fda5d-8dbd-578f-ba57-15366c54f032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce190ce-0319-5382-a9e1-b39ca5b6c857" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252359} :answer-field/id #uuid "b25237fb-1e27-5e8c-8801-00721dc99e4b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253115}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e6c40bb9-f96b-5776-9878-956cda7f4ab8" :question/math-academy-id "q-117602" :question/problem "Which expression gives all of the zeros of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$  Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate the zeros of \n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe zeros of this function are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the zeros of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x-\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =n\\pi  \\\\ 3x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the zeros are $x=\\frac{\\pi }{12}+\\frac{n\\pi }{3},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203963 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "75e981a4-ba75-5db2-81ab-b321953cc225" :knowledge-point/title "Minima of Transformed Sine or Cosine Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a6315d9-4c5b-52e3-98df-900d3c444852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{10}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250644} {:answer/id #uuid "50caab44-146e-5114-bb63-a98abf44bbf0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251216} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8a89f1-cd1e-5d53-97df-4d4822adf932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251602} {:answer/id #uuid "8cef8dea-f51e-5b0b-821f-4305afcb5ffc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{2\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251744} {:answer/id #uuid "a7938484-56c7-5ea3-8eac-d9b734d000ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{10}+2n\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d8a89f1-cd1e-5d53-97df-4d4822adf932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251602} :answer-field/id #uuid "f788a829-1171-5925-a3c0-50bd4a554edb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253243}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21457735-cc06-5dc4-85da-719f77cfb1e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-117609" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ Which expression gives all of the values of $x$ at which $f(x)$ attains its minimum value? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "First, let's start by defining a new variable $X$ as\n\n$$\nX=5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to calculate $X$-values at the minima of\n\n$$\nf(x)=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡X.\n$$\n\nThe $X$-values at the minima are given by the following expression:\n\n$$\nX=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi \n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the $x$-values at the minima of our original function, we rewrite $X$ in terms of the original variable $x$ and solve the resulting equation for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 5x+\\frac{\\pi }{2} & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2n\\pi  \\\\ 5x & =-\\pi +2n\\pi  \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the values of $x$ that minimize $f(x)$ are given by $x=-\\frac{\\pi }{5}+\\frac{2n\\pi }{5},$ where $n$ is any integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6506f7e3-4c69-5352-a09d-262adee65e59" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Period of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a24ca73-da2f-5891-880a-ef75f6b9593c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250779} {:answer/id #uuid "7150f51b-e3ac-5d81-a7f7-48ee5bc0bba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251481} {:answer/id #uuid "78cfaed6-e561-54a3-bf1b-803861939fec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251555} {:answer/id #uuid "8a4ab11c-2641-522c-977a-0adc46be731f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251718} {:answer/id #uuid "f94e9a48-ac9c-5839-8dce-557d1258a588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252735}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a24ca73-da2f-5891-880a-ef75f6b9593c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250779} :answer-field/id #uuid "adab447b-5cdd-5271-ba5a-532032cbadc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253107}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7daa9d90-3598-5b04-bfa2-8fabe3b59b76" :question/math-academy-id "q-117659" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)+6.$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=3.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253386 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129099 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6506f7e3-4c69-5352-a09d-262adee65e59" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Period of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2430686f-6354-5ae3-a56a-86797016a196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250867} {:answer/id #uuid "84c30401-b464-5d98-8a9b-267f533cca26" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251667} {:answer/id #uuid "97869399-0a57-5928-b446-b55279c01911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853} {:answer/id #uuid "a5606c09-671a-5dc4-abd2-15172f36984e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251979} {:answer/id #uuid "ab67f75e-df9d-5d97-89f6-287ab3d2483f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97869399-0a57-5928-b446-b55279c01911" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251853} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f5b3662-7c4a-554a-a3fa-0abb3a6d9754" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252975}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dc8f3fd2-a7ad-59ae-81ee-947a33e17f52" :question/math-academy-id "q-117661" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $f(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that\n\n$$\nf(x)=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{8x-\\pi }{4})=-3\\operatorname{tan}⁡(2x-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, $B=2,$ and so the period is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253397 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8854eaba-96c7-5ea8-a071-7ff7c954f943" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Asymptotes of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16b544f9-45ed-52b5-8a10-492ef7f4a669" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250750} {:answer/id #uuid "54f09443-1da3-52d4-89f7-68f073373233" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251250} {:answer/id #uuid "87855055-3d0a-5377-8e83-180ea0f68c3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {120}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251691} {:answer/id #uuid "9325c435-93c8-5008-832e-ae2119d01416" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {90}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251814} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2ec20d-773b-513c-b262-9a0a45f8791c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eb2ec20d-773b-513c-b262-9a0a45f8791c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=n\\cdot {60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252614} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f9041f-2602-5a62-b684-748173858460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253118}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "41c041cc-5fd3-5771-8502-8b1349fc86e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-117692" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function  $f(x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-5,$  where \n$x$ is measured in degrees? Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=n\\cdot {180}^{∘},\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $2\\operatorname{cot}⁡(3x)-5,$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(3x).$ This gives\n\n$$\n3x=n\\cdot {180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x & =n\\cdot {180}^{∘} \\\\ x & =\\frac{n\\cdot {180}^{∘}}{3} \\\\ x & =n\\cdot {60}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=n\\cdot {60}^{∘},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129101 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8854eaba-96c7-5ea8-a071-7ff7c954f943" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a General Expression for the Asymptotes of a Transformed Tangent or Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "192cc94d-2bdc-5aa5-8395-3038aa3ee599" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{n\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250773} {:answer/id #uuid "4831ea5f-e09b-527b-af85-515fffa0b3b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251154} {:answer/id #uuid "5214d5d3-d621-543f-8d56-e6f7af5a61c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251225} {:answer/id #uuid "65c18de8-50f1-510b-be3e-67e3e14593c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251389} {:answer/id #uuid "9ee22af7-fed1-5570-8b02-e7f32f959eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{n\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65c18de8-50f1-510b-be3e-67e3e14593c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251389} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c602f0c-b42d-53d5-bc8e-aa3b17f2cc1f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a30a0cb-a192-58f4-b341-cb115dd8b12f" :question/math-academy-id "q-117698" :question/problem "Which of the following expressions gives all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4})?$ Assume that $n$ is an integer." :question/worked-solution "The asymptotes of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ are given by the expression\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is an integer.\n\nTo find the asymptotes of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ we replace the $x$ on the left-hand side of the above with $(4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ This gives\n\n$$\n4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi .\n$$\n\nSolving for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4x+\\frac{\\pi }{4} & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi  \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\ 4x & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}+n\\pi  \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the expression giving all the vertical asymptotes is $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}+\\frac{n\\pi }{4},$ where $n$ is an integer."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078598 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4abe11e-febf-5461-96ee-bca8a7713bdf" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Definite Integral of a Piecewise Linear Function Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00a25b06-d824-5579-bf60-2d2c4e45d9a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250556} {:answer/id #uuid "7e767a49-c1bd-5566-8a4d-80d682dab01f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251615} {:answer/id #uuid "97a511f7-2a8d-5737-948b-58070b8fd816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251856} {:answer/id #uuid "b356ac98-e823-5f19-b0ba-a27840a19fb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252123} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfb57c0-e3ea-50c7-b3dc-b1dba4050fe2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252499}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00a25b06-d824-5579-bf60-2d2c4e45d9a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250556} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a13fb81-d7bb-5742-9e3c-de58bdbfcea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252965}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b8467112-b8ce-58b8-acef-c1b741ea25db" :question/math-academy-id "q-117712" :question/problem "![](images/45/45e506d3b6d3a2ca1a9b16a061f5c081e10b8c578796d7d4a1dfab6e6f793520.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x),$ shown above, consists of three line segments. Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We divide the interval of integration into $4$ regions: the line segment for $x\\in [-5,-3],$ the line segment for $x\\in [-3,-1],$ the line segment for $x\\in (-1,1),$ and the line segment for $x\\in [1,4].$ Therefore, we can write the required integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={{{\\int }_{-5}^{-3}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{1}}+{{{\\int }_{-3}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{2}}+{{{\\int }_{-1}^{1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{{A}_{3}}+{{{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x}_{⏟}}_{-{A}_{4}}\n$$\n\n- The $1$st integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-5,-3].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $2$nd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [-3,-1].$ The corresponding region is a right triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{-1}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{2}=-2.\n$$\n\n- The $3$rd integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in (-1,1).$ The corresponding region is a square, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{3} & ={s}^{2} \\\\  & ={2}^{2} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- The $4$th integral represents the signed area between the curve and the $x$-axis, where $x\\in [1,4].$ The corresponding region is a triangle, so we find the area using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}_{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}(bh) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(3\\cdot 3) \\\\  & =4.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we have\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-{A}_{4}=-4.5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{4}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={A}_{1}-{A}_{2}+{A}_{3}-{A}_{4} \\\\  & =2-2+4-4.5 \\\\  & =-0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253406 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17099881-d7b9-54d9-a29a-4f5f28561cd3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola with Vertex at the Origin Given Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0768a482-1a2f-5a34-8c85-9a34c76622bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250614} {:answer/id #uuid "2808b25a-1672-5fbc-8379-8016452a80f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250897} {:answer/id #uuid "91bb1123-01ba-56c5-beca-b049f55736e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-5x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251798} {:answer/id #uuid "abfb8433-1bf6-509a-aa93-e166d63ab29f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252045} {:answer/id #uuid "af0b99cf-a018-507e-a035-e93e9ec63d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af0b99cf-a018-507e-a035-e93e9ec63d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-7x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252078} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef8a9cff-d5bd-58c8-adc0-f7db16811aa1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253224}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e14f2af-b637-53ee-90cb-4edef45fef4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-11772" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and passes through the point $(-7,-7).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(0,0)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}=4px.\n$$\n\nSince we know the given parabola passes through the point $(-7,-7),$ we can substitute $x=-7$ and $y=-7$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ (-7)^{2} & =4p(-7) \\\\ 49 & =-28p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4px \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{7}{4})x \\\\ {y}^{2} & =-7x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253410 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083152 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b19d6e0-8a12-525d-8c80-ac2de8080830" :knowledge-point/title "The Period of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2935cc2d-27c9-58b3-96a6-79c8386f3a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250904} {:answer/id #uuid "54137cf8-14cc-55d2-a3b0-586abb911a1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251241} {:answer/id #uuid "80c578dc-923e-50b4-8174-f24b0bd436b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251638} {:answer/id #uuid "92a04e97-de9c-5fe7-b1bc-3c133f09c650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251808} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ed5eb0-3633-589d-acca-01d3eccf8929" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92a04e97-de9c-5fe7-b1bc-3c133f09c650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251808} :answer-field/id #uuid "27623253-c44d-540a-b82d-ad67ac207865" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b44fb065-7fc5-53f5-aa2b-171c7b74a7ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-117752" :question/problem "Calculate the period of the function $y=-5\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{x}{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Given a function of the form $y=A\\operatorname{csc}⁡(Bx+C)+D,$ the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{B}.$\n\nIn our case, we have that $B=\\frac{1}{6}.$ Therefore, the period is $\\frac{2\\pi }{(\\frac{1}{6})}=12\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253411 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "018b900e-eac6-5ee8-98ca-c0981f9c358a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250568} {:answer/id #uuid "36b69884-e0f8-5a35-a86f-6504c958fffb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251003} {:answer/id #uuid "6c61d383-b5d6-55e5-89aa-69e7219ae6e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251442} {:answer/id #uuid "9c25fc1b-e751-5f4d-a4ea-2c79ebdd5611" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251901} {:answer/id #uuid "b600ef2d-7430-52c3-9a6b-33649cc4dade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b600ef2d-7430-52c3-9a6b-33649cc4dade" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252153} :answer-field/id #uuid "56c419e4-fed3-5638-9bd2-e528284bfe38" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4844153a-cf84-57ba-8cb5-907264c75fda" :question/math-academy-id "q-11779" :question/problem "![](images/b0/b09f63373bba1bb796f1832a4c7aa33ec220186e75bb84ccc79dcc46b662636e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is downward-opening has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,-12).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,-12),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-12$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(-12) \\\\ 16 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(-\\frac{1}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-\\frac{4}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184236 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af228cc4-8be2-5c16-8537-4e5dab4e2ff4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Tangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "34eca68d-7384-5ac4-aae3-9fd3ad9100c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398167} {:answer/id #uuid "ebf81549-b73c-5aa8-ae6f-2eb529e3d650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398168} {:answer/id #uuid "4b365ba9-edc9-5d4f-9d0c-a4e1280cad8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398169} {:answer/id #uuid "95065041-df6a-55e2-ba59-eb39e1ef3c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398170} {:answer/id #uuid "4041d9b8-a50e-5114-a489-9911977efeb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ebf81549-b73c-5aa8-ae6f-2eb529e3d650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398168} :answer-field/id #uuid "b20222d5-4d8b-5ef3-b5e6-cdd257fd9b17" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398166}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5ecbe368-922d-5a9f-956d-5a27886e10df" :question/math-academy-id "q-117796" :question/problem "![](images/92/9278723975d70fec73e313d0e164908ddd339231cb74f75b07c2edce14680111.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{2\\pi }{5}-(-\\frac{2\\pi }{5})=\\frac{4\\pi }{5}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{tan}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{5})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the tangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $\\frac{1}{2}$ unit up. Hence, $D=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{7}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5x}{4})+\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dac9c72b-c23f-56fa-b939-6e3b56d3a13b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Stretch and Vertical Shift of a Transformed Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dd86b80a-f69a-5bee-9e25-9498124a3b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398174} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4cbf13-3436-5ca9-a85e-d79666e97f91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398175} {:answer/id #uuid "a192845d-5444-593f-a294-b8369a05e099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398176} {:answer/id #uuid "e570139b-a17c-5e71-8fdc-ac883bda1ab2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398177} {:answer/id #uuid "c337dc59-1dd1-5197-98b9-3c6fcbf08e62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a192845d-5444-593f-a294-b8369a05e099" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398176} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b387afd-4a67-578b-8db3-0662089f213d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "554b2923-f03c-51dd-9817-6e0a2f8280b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-117799" :question/problem "![](images/c9/c9ed24a7fa07d6c29e4125c8428fb61a42fc969b99f101c4737bdedf9bf036ff.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx)+D,$ where $B$ and $D$ are constants. What is $B+D?$" :question/worked-solution "The period $T$ of the function is\n\n$$\nT=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}-0=\\frac{4\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe formula for the period $T$ of the function $y=A\\operatorname{cot}⁡(Bx+C)+D$ is given by \n\n$$\nT=\\frac{\\pi }{B}.\n$$\n\nApplying the formula of the period, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{4\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{B}\\,⟹\\,B=\\frac{\\pi }{(\\frac{4\\pi }{3})}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nThe parameter $D$ represents the vertical shift of the cotangent function. We see that the function has been translated by $3$ units down. Hence, $D=-3.$\n\nTherefore, $B+D=\\frac{3}{4}+(-3)=-\\frac{9}{4}.$\n\nFinally, the equation of the curve is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{cot}⁡(\\frac{3x}{4})-3.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083155 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5085a30f-a347-5ba8-80fd-102e4e0f37ce" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Extrema of a Transformed Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ef0ad4-504c-5ff5-b6b1-d2d3e980875f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250635} {:answer/id #uuid "298bec1a-ff16-5540-a476-6b3fca320aa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250908} {:answer/id #uuid "2dc5b675-6831-54be-906b-a9a1b71d2899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250944} {:answer/id #uuid "885e749a-e263-5b04-8671-ccd884576931" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251703} {:answer/id #uuid "a365c952-0f98-5c32-87e5-0e635c62c1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2dc5b675-6831-54be-906b-a9a1b71d2899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250944} :answer-field/id #uuid "94db1709-d36e-5a1b-b01a-c474544f7620" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "97fbbd90-72d5-5259-afd8-7a25639443c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-117805" :question/problem "What is the value of $f(x)=4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1$ at its local maximum points?" :question/worked-solution "On a negative branch of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\le -1.\n$$\n\nThe points where $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-1$ are local maximums of the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$ \n\nHorizontal shifts and stretches have no effect on the range. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1.\n$$\n\nMultiplying the above inequality by $4$ and adding $1$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -1 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\le -4 \\\\  & 4\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+1\\le -4+1 \\\\  & f(x)\\le -3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=-3$ at the local maximum points."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253420 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096692 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9a34b79-65ff-5f7b-a8ee-5f19424fd9b4" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d74283e-5ccb-5927-b7c2-7b6dc7348729" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250940} {:answer/id #uuid "49d88741-afb9-5f1a-89bc-fa72f184e234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251167} {:answer/id #uuid "53cc6df8-4837-502d-9577-0769a855428d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{2}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251238} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e45b1d-95f4-5298-b7f6-badd3269cd0d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-\\frac{4}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252418} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaf0ab8-8236-5a51-9644-727fd4c758d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252495}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcaf0ab8-8236-5a51-9644-727fd4c758d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252495} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf9081df-efa4-55bb-b2fa-1ac432b3fbd4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253137}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73e0be4b-b8c0-5d2c-baec-36cb996c3031" :question/math-academy-id "q-11781" :question/problem "![](images/e5/e588a9250583251ba2fd85b789eb3a856970fb6e7044289257a4ed97e3bbf1a0.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the parabola is upward-opening, has its vertex at $(0,0),$ and passes through the point $(4,6).$\n\nFor any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe know that the given parabola passes through the point $(4,6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {4}^{2} & =4p(6) \\\\ 16 & =24p \\\\ p & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{2}{3})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{8}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184149 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "84355770-5926-5be6-9b9f-c13bb05599ae" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Horizontal Shift of a Horizontally Stretched Tangent or Cotangent Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1382a0da-6af2-58cd-a188-1d157bd20cd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250722} {:answer/id #uuid "255f49c4-fc05-50ed-8b6d-c7d105680f5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{14}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250875} {:answer/id #uuid "70d6cf7f-09b3-54e7-91e5-21216ebc3505" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251475} {:answer/id #uuid "8f7c0e22-c4ef-5f16-9e47-d3a2ab3e0908" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251770} {:answer/id #uuid "958902e2-d4fc-583d-abca-b3667fecc3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251838}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "958902e2-d4fc-583d-abca-b3667fecc3aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251838} :answer-field/id #uuid "0dd471e5-3d02-59ea-ae43-9700669b6052" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252832}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3de18967-1c79-524e-b070-80260fcd64fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-117888" :question/problem "![](images/58/5819e0605dacfb190c2767c4f50a671eb592832aa6a3a1970a8bcdff2790fd19.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the function $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C),$ where $C$ is a constant. Given that $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ what is the value of $C?$" :question/worked-solution "The parameter $C$ represents a horizontal shift of the function.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the function by factoring the horizontal stretch factor, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x+C) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $x+\\frac{C}{3}$ indicates that the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3(x+\\frac{C}{3}))$ is a horizontal translation of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x).$ Comparing the two graphs below allows us to determine the amount of this shift.\n\n![](images/ad/ad5bf0001a37cf5bebf3288ffbf1fa6bb69747f28f8a81d08d0ffab8223e1707.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that our curve has been obtained by shifting $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(3x)$ by $\\frac{\\pi }{21}-0=\\frac{\\pi }{21}$ units to the right. Therefore\n\n$$\n\\frac{C}{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{21}\\,⟹\\,C=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.\n$$\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\pi }{2}<C<\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ and $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ is in this interval, the value of $C$ is $-\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253430 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096693 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "281514e9-6e16-5b21-ba4e-503991d811c2" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29bfd946-372a-5444-a539-b716d9cbb951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250911} {:answer/id #uuid "315d3443-669e-53c8-825c-4dd3c15813b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250972} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6adb72-8b7c-5af0-8717-9d8ed3666c4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251572} {:answer/id #uuid "96e517f3-26bc-5e99-8b69-bc21a87de470" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=-5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251847} {:answer/id #uuid "97998a1b-9445-5245-b410-a419f925511e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=3y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29bfd946-372a-5444-a539-b716d9cbb951" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}=5y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250911} :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6c6253-24f7-571b-b3ec-a0a27c5878db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252942}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0b80741-e06b-5c43-b887-c4210becc6d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-11792" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(0,0)$ and which passes through the point $(-5,5).$" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola whose vertex is $(0,0),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}=4py\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant.\n\nWe're told that the given parabola passes through the point $(-5,5),$ so we can substitute $x=-5$ and $y=5$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ (-5)^{2} & =4p(5) \\\\ 25 & =20p \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =4py \\\\ {x}^{2} & =4(\\frac{5}{4})y \\\\ {x}^{2} & =5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "007e5875-12bb-5469-bee2-9e308d1913ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=8(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250555} {:answer/id #uuid "7cbb2c97-6267-5a4e-ad5c-4eecd966ac12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-4(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251593} {:answer/id #uuid "9adbb64e-58b5-520b-a756-8c681f0041b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=4(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251883} {:answer/id #uuid "c7eb3c43-5a45-5db8-8e81-b8791ebc8549" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+2)^{2}=8(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252323} {:answer/id #uuid "fd5df63c-eb61-59d1-b092-73a48046cece" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=-8(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252773}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "007e5875-12bb-5469-bee2-9e308d1913ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-2)^{2}=8(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250555} :answer-field/id #uuid "deee0397-808f-5aa4-bf00-34e759fcff77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bbd1ed97-ed69-50ed-938c-aebfe4625b89" :question/math-academy-id "q-11793" :question/problem "![](images/9e/9eb2efa6d60dc4bd1d7915900e60be8a891f824799ed6ada8f2753fa71bb01c6.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(-2,2)$ and passes through the point $(6,10).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-2,2),$ we substitute $h=-2$ and $k=2$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(6,10),$ so we can substitute $x=6$ and $y=10$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (10-2)^{2} & =4p(6+2) \\\\ 64 & =32p \\\\ p & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-2)^{2} & =4p(x+2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =4(2)(x+2) \\\\ (y-2)^{2} & =8(x+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=2$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253431 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096705 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "783ca59b-0242-584c-ae47-004a0eb03817" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given the Vertex and Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "067eb9b1-51de-5ef8-b70e-3b3e8cda0c87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250606} {:answer/id #uuid "13e8d2d2-417b-57ab-9155-6e7000efdb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250725} {:answer/id #uuid "33afd160-c8ab-5044-8b0b-8f8d3675e347" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-1)^{2}=-8(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250983} {:answer/id #uuid "abc58071-d220-590e-a965-1da2251fffb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-4(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252041} {:answer/id #uuid "b0209be0-b337-582a-a85e-98717e955cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13e8d2d2-417b-57ab-9155-6e7000efdb81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-8(x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250725} :answer-field/id #uuid "26ea5a01-cd1c-58e7-985e-fe58df8c683e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252875}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f5a78323-0f8e-5b1d-8bfd-600ef8cd7e86" :question/math-academy-id "q-11794" :question/problem "Find the equation of the left-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,4)$ and passes through the point $(-1,8).$" :question/worked-solution "The general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,4),$ we substitute $h=1$ and $k=4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(-1,8),$ so we can substitute $x=-1$ and $y=8$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (8-4)^{2} & =4p(-1-1) \\\\ 16 & =-8p \\\\ p & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4(-2)(x-1) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-8(x-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253433 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e367aafb-2474-5dfc-a45f-be3a5ea76942" :knowledge-point/title "Approximating the Solution to an Initial Value Problem Using Euler's Method With Two Steps"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39db5d0b-282e-51f5-ba10-f632141269a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251030} {:answer/id #uuid "3ebebed7-1156-50dd-b495-b103e7023a2b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251076} {:answer/id #uuid "4c013829-c4ae-5437-9bf0-99f40f9385a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251182} {:answer/id #uuid "52d065ca-e0ac-5430-9852-489865f7ecbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251229} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ef0794-a68a-5b8b-99e8-2aa6c12731f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252324}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7ef0794-a68a-5b8b-99e8-2aa6c12731f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252324} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b9357f7-8572-5aa9-ae8a-d0c001dbf416" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "af0427a6-d3d0-5efe-950b-968ece9492c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-117942" :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2},\\,y(0)=2\n$$\n\nUse Euler's method with two steps of equal size to approximate $y(2).$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that we are given $y(0)$ and asked to find $y(2).$ The distance from $x=0$ to $x=2$ is $2,$ and since we are asked to use two steps of equal size, our step size must be \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-0}{2}=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, let's proceed with Euler's method. We'll start by creating a table that includes the initial condition data $y(0)=2.$\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\n**Step 1.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the first row) and the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2}=\\frac{{2}^{2}\\cdot 0}{2}=0 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=0\\cdot 1=0 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=0+1=1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+0=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this data to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\frac{{y}^{2}x}{2}=\\frac{{2}^{2}\\cdot 1}{2}=2 \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=2\\cdot 1=2 \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+1=2 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+2=4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $0$ | $2$ | $0$ | $0$ |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $2$ | $2$ |\n| $2$ | $4$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n \n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y(2)≈4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253446 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d8acb62-1689-516f-9882-4c9dda55e1c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251328} {:answer/id #uuid "77de82a0-e73e-5de8-a117-54e53271cdb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251544} {:answer/id #uuid "7a6921a9-f376-5914-8a9e-5b2f42ba5a37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y+4)^{2}=3x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251571} {:answer/id #uuid "95781b19-83eb-5875-9326-f078ecb9c39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251837} {:answer/id #uuid "fc5538d8-0f12-5dc5-a2c9-99178bd82c8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=-2(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252764}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95781b19-83eb-5875-9326-f078ecb9c39d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{y}^{2}=3(x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251837} :answer-field/id #uuid "9bf11644-eb3c-55f3-8b1b-a01c321a67d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "157ae011-ca8e-518d-b6fc-d2c2017281bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-11805" :question/problem "![](images/a1/a16f3f2ba78b5aef180c4331d1dd99f37340d2c6d842c1596e35445f1dbf34c5.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the right. The parabola has its vertex at $(-4,0)$ and passes through the point $(8,6).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(-4,0),$ we substitute $h=-4$ and $k=0$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(8,6),$ so we can substitute $x=8$ and $y=6$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {6}^{2} & =4p(8+4) \\\\ 36 & =48p \\\\ p & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2} & =4p(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =4(\\frac{3}{4})(x+4) \\\\ {y}^{2} & =3(x+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the right since $p=\\frac{3}{4}$ is positive."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253454 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096704 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc86c375-c2c4-56b7-a207-0abfc9709f6a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Equation of a Left or Right-Opening Parabola Given a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08b7a1e2-2234-5972-a128-08fb39f06dbb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=5(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250630} {:answer/id #uuid "b60bb7a8-52f6-584f-a4bf-3e844e94e661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=3(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252154} {:answer/id #uuid "c52f456e-d64e-5f46-a9e4-84edfe0766c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-6)^{2}=-5(x-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252299} {:answer/id #uuid "d7e3e02b-032b-5e7e-9f66-142cdfdd6a61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-3(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252466} {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d962a-57d2-545b-a36a-897c8ba80fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-5(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fb3d962a-57d2-545b-a36a-897c8ba80fcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(y-4)^{2}=-5(x-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252756} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f980315-6664-5b6c-9a9a-9c681494e4b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa256900-e0ea-52f1-985a-4c3b8bdded9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11811" :question/problem "![](images/ec/ecf128d341a8a568ade4abaa2a59e1083d2c436fdb3d2a0b4b642fb7e85cee63.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "From the diagram we see that the arms of the parabola open to the left. The parabola has its vertex at $(6,4)$ and passes through the point $(1,9).$\n\nThe general standard equation of a parabola whose vertex is $(h,k)$ and whose arms open right or left is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-k)^{2} & =4p(x-h).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we know that the given parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(6,4),$ we substitute $h=6$ and $k=4$ into the equation above and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also know that the given parabola passes through the point $(1,9),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=9$ into the equation above and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6) \\\\ (9-4)^{2} & =4p(1-6) \\\\ 25 & =-20p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-4)^{2} & =4p(x-6) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{5}{4})(x-6) \\\\ (y-4)^{2} & =-5(x-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the arms of the parabola open to the left since $p=-\\frac{5}{4}$ is negative."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253456 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18683fc1-6e6f-552f-b3d8-1a598e647afe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250768} {:answer/id #uuid "afd8104c-d61b-5874-bc43-96a15275c763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252087} {:answer/id #uuid "b3372c5e-1b3d-526f-a2ff-bdb9fc2ba5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252121} {:answer/id #uuid "c7189c96-8126-52ba-9ab5-81ef9869fbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252312} {:answer/id #uuid "db77b71c-5ce2-5ae9-9c3c-53b2ac47a7d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "d=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252480}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7189c96-8126-52ba-9ab5-81ef9869fbe6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252312} :answer-field/id #uuid "485e4678-0882-55ac-8cb1-e26d4e0dafbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8477c0d3-7e97-5832-b4f8-9e356450f509" :question/math-academy-id "q-118114" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{{d}^{2}-3d}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)}=\\frac{2}{d-3}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(d,\\,d(d-3),\\,d-3)=d(d-3).\n$$\n\n- The values $d=0$ and $d=3$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)} & =\\frac{2}{d-3} \\\\ d(d-3)\\cdot (\\frac{4}{d}+\\frac{6}{d(d-3)}) & =d(d-3)\\cdot \\frac{2}{d-3} \\\\ \\frac{4d(d-3)}{d}+\\frac{6d(d-3)}{d(d-3)} & =\\frac{2d(d-3)}{d-3} \\\\ \\frac{4\\cdot \\cancel{d}\\cdot (d-3)}{\\cancel{d}}+\\frac{6\\cdot \\cancel{d(d-3)}}{\\cancel{d(d-3)}} & =\\frac{2d\\cdot \\cancel{(d-3)}}{\\cancel{d-3}} \\\\ 4(d-3)+6 & =2d\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $d,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(d-3)+6 & =2d \\\\ 4d-6 & =2d \\\\ 2d & =6 \\\\ d & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $d=3$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253457 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083241 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "89dc22a6-dd98-544c-8232-5043fac2bd3d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Rational Equations by Factoring a Quadratic Denominator With No Constant Term"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0801c677-febc-5048-b4a9-bd16e29096aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250620} {:answer/id #uuid "259a4b59-97e1-5b2a-ae19-ec0054e36543" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250878} {:answer/id #uuid "9b65b064-032f-5a74-8b4d-b5fd5feeec61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251892} {:answer/id #uuid "def82426-7548-5608-afb8-c119d37b39c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252518} {:answer/id #uuid "f83cbd43-a1c5-5373-b93c-4ff388a4a312" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "v=-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "def82426-7548-5608-afb8-c119d37b39c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solutions" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252518} :answer-field/id #uuid "6942e994-071a-50b2-a350-0106e069e5db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "686ef7d4-296f-572a-a3ca-d6455dccd76c" :question/math-academy-id "q-118117" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{{v}^{2}+4v}=\\frac{3}{v}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the quadratic denominator, giving us the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}=\\frac{3}{v}.\n$$\n\nWe now make the following observations:\n\n- The least common denominator of our equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(v+4,\\,v(v+4),\\,v)=v(v+4).\n$$\n\n- The values $v=0$ and $v=-4$ cannot be solutions to our equation. These values make at least one denominator in our original equation equal zero, and division by zero is undefined.\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of the equation by the least common denominator, we can simplify it as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3}{v} \\\\ v(v+4)\\cdot (\\frac{6}{v+4}+\\frac{12}{v(v+4)}) & =v(v+4)\\cdot \\frac{3}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v(v+4)}{v+4}+\\frac{12v(v+4)}{v(v+4)} & =\\frac{3v(v+4)}{v} \\\\ \\frac{6v\\cdot \\cancel{(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v+4}}+\\frac{12\\cdot \\cancel{v(v+4)}}{\\cancel{v(v+4)}} & =\\frac{3\\cdot \\cancel{v}\\cdot (v+4)}{\\cancel{v}} \\\\ 6v+12 & =3(v+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $v,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6v+12 & =3(v+4) \\\\ 6v+12 & =3v+12 \\\\ 3v & =0 \\\\ v & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, since $v=0$  is not a valid solution, our equation has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253466 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "169c4d0d-a974-5ab0-ab10-ece44e9bf719" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=4(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250749} {:answer/id #uuid "27b81da8-ffe4-5749-9d5c-518f42d88756" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=2(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250893} {:answer/id #uuid "34c9b005-e169-5610-83db-29fce1448453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250995} {:answer/id #uuid "7bee81ac-628e-5cc9-9189-39eb038e571c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251585} {:answer/id #uuid "d5bedb93-90d7-53ff-82ed-889cadbd470e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-4(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "34c9b005-e169-5610-83db-29fce1448453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=2(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250995} :answer-field/id #uuid "72c319bd-e1d9-5c37-820b-e6a962767d54" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253008}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ca3b7f51-a5a6-56e7-8463-29fa6d537419" :question/math-academy-id "q-11822" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(-1,-4)$ and which passes through the point $(1,-2).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-1,-4).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-(-1))^{2} & =4p(y-(-4)) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(1,-2),$ so we can substitute $x=1$ and $y=-2$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (1+1)^{2} & =4p(-2+4) \\\\ 4 & =8p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+1)^{2} & =4p(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{2})(y+4) \\\\ (x+1)^{2} & =2(y+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253467 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096845 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c269944-636a-50f2-bfc9-c32a7b2e7ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Product Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46092d6a-5809-530b-96aa-d18ca741a149" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251132} {:answer/id #uuid "9449d2c4-40c8-5886-a8f8-d377e92abd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+10){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251828} {:answer/id #uuid "9fcc1d83-99af-5f5a-b373-703da0e18e92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251929} {:answer/id #uuid "a8022752-cd20-552c-942e-08b64f288227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9x{e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252002} {:answer/id #uuid "af7653ed-b253-50e2-9a68-8c72c94c9870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9(x+1){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9449d2c4-40c8-5886-a8f8-d377e92abd71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9x+10){e}^{9x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251828} :answer-field/id #uuid "2531d2a5-3c0e-51d6-8cfe-475ebfb26445" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab66beff-1db7-5265-a536-ea4d30aeb123" :question/math-academy-id "q-11824" :question/problem "Given that $y=(x+1){e}^{9x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "This question requires the product rule. Let $u(x)=x+1$ and $v(x)={e}^{9x}.$ Then\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=1,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=9{e}^{9x}.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)+u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =(1)({e}^{9x})+(x+1)(9{e}^{9x}) \\\\  & ={e}^{9x}+9(x+1){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(1+9(x+1)){e}^{9x} \\\\  & =(9x+10){e}^{9x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253469 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06cec48d-e68e-5bcc-8270-08fe2e886d9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}=y-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250608} {:answer/id #uuid "8e6cc6fa-f362-599f-ade6-ae329e081e03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=2(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251760} {:answer/id #uuid "bb75a7c2-9da9-57cf-a080-80bcbe9c27dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-2(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252202} {:answer/id #uuid "c9b6a622-8399-502c-875d-4a9c8d81675b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=-(y+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252339} {:answer/id #uuid "d07899d6-980a-597d-8721-0d4dbbcd9959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=y+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d07899d6-980a-597d-8721-0d4dbbcd9959" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-3)^{2}=y+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252396} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cb337ec-659a-584a-b940-40e7dd1bcc66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253081}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a291d23e-d654-581b-aa5a-090e3a807669" :question/math-academy-id "q-11827" :question/problem "Find the equation of the upward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(3,-5)$ and which passes through the point $(0,4).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(3,-5).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y-(-5)) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(0,4),$ so we can substitute $x=0$ and $y=4$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (0-3)^{2} & =4p(4+5) \\\\ 9 & =36p \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2} & =4p(y+5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =4(\\frac{1}{4})(y+5) \\\\ (x-3)^{2} & =y+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096695 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "29e2083d-fe76-5a35-9c3a-7e6ad19ff968" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Passing Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0204d1a4-979b-5e5a-91e8-16da198d96f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+1)^{2}=-7(y+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250572} {:answer/id #uuid "03bd375b-9e1f-597d-b5e9-e0b4bc54bc30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250582} {:answer/id #uuid "12e32eca-626a-5a03-9f08-308e2110d81a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250714} {:answer/id #uuid "7de8efb9-9a5f-5339-a2b1-15a8967978ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251610} {:answer/id #uuid "efdf1fa1-0772-50d6-9d54-6cbe5ab65088" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=3(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252652}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7de8efb9-9a5f-5339-a2b1-15a8967978ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-1)^{2}=-7(y-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251610} :answer-field/id #uuid "8ee73095-9b6e-56be-abd4-9d4c75590945" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6128a71e-f744-5ae8-9df3-81f671cec4ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-11833" :question/problem "Find the equation of the downward-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(1,1)$ and which passes through the point $(-6,-6).$" :question/worked-solution "For any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(1,1).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(-6,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=-6$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (-6-1)^{2} & =4p(-6-1) \\\\ 49 & =-28p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{7}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-1)^{2} & =4p(y-1) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{7}{4})(y-1) \\\\ (x-1)^{2} & =-7(y-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096694 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a991a3b1-6de7-5978-9dd6-cb9eb3f183a6" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Equation of a Parabola With Non-Origin Vertex Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a2abbd7-97f8-5e38-941b-3ada6db5798c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251035} {:answer/id #uuid "8787dc86-a84a-5867-8afa-8d199ba176b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{8}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251694} {:answer/id #uuid "b0735a9e-9190-50e7-a39d-2d9c6954469a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252095} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc062ed-3073-5920-bc31-ce60d55001a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-6)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252207} {:answer/id #uuid "c2ea3214-bf6f-5b3f-a080-c4a79666da78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252276}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0735a9e-9190-50e7-a39d-2d9c6954469a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+4)^{2}=-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252095} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d36355d-1e8b-5128-984f-3296bcb5a4b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f807a98f-968e-51ff-a643-2a1ff4add6c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-11835" :question/problem "![](images/28/2819ceb4214f8df19ef349794c243771a1080bccf0bf2bbdbdbca9be67d4fbdb.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "We know that for any upward or downward-opening parabola with its vertex at $(h,k),$ the general standard equation is given by\n\n$$\n(x-h)^{2}=4p(y-k).\n$$\n\nHere, the vertex has coordinates $(h,k)=(-4,6).$ Substituting these into the general standard equation, we get\n\n$$\n(x+4)^{2}=4p(y-6).\n$$\n\nWe are left to find the value of $p.$ We know that the given parabola also passes through the point $(4,-6),$ so we can substitute $x=4$ and $y=-6$ into the standard equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (4+4)^{2} & =4p(-6-6) \\\\ 64 & =-48p \\\\ p & =-\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the parabola is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+4)^{2} & =4p(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =4(-\\frac{4}{3})(y-6) \\\\ (x+4)^{2} & =-\\frac{16}{3}(y-6).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253474 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059763 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "64edd709-3f6c-59e1-ae1f-bca25eca0483" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection of a Sine Curve Over the Horizontal Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4755537d-9558-58e3-b1fb-e15adf27ad35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(x-\\frac{1}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251146} {:answer/id #uuid "4f6bddc5-a5cd-5875-abbb-9f22efb60464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(\\frac{x}{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251203} {:answer/id #uuid "6b736f08-32b3-5af6-a82e-5816f1c230a0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}-sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251431} {:answer/id #uuid "790ade44-b249-5c2c-8b0f-5e62399db5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251558} {:answer/id #uuid "cdec9314-5fd3-5b0f-8a26-a6c75f8c9e96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡x-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "790ade44-b249-5c2c-8b0f-5e62399db5ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\frac{1}{4}sin⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251558} :answer-field/id #uuid "5268f851-1f31-5f10-a461-a1a9a49c25a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5a416a1-a537-5d81-9140-46bc32e0a299" :question/math-academy-id "q-118350" :question/problem "![](images/a0/a065146d50b16eade39c758b59e13994272cf7180aaa662008a1865e4160421e.png)\n\nWhat is the equation of the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/46/46e8f92e758c7f4643b64c05d0b457f7b99b6723ddf9be9cd63da28079b89618.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, compress it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ to give $y=-\\frac{1}{4}sin⁡x.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-\\frac{1}{4}sin⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398186 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85090bdf-4f1b-5f79-a751-48521ad98895" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection of a Sine or Cosine Curve Over the Vertical Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bddbf7dd-8781-50a4-b250-66fd8e233371" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}sin⁡(-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398188} {:answer/id #uuid "7f35edde-ed58-55bc-8274-99043f11903d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(-x)-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398189} {:answer/id #uuid "e98140d9-64d1-5b2b-87eb-6cb37aace0e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(-\\frac{x}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398190} {:answer/id #uuid "1866cc81-a61e-52b4-828e-7f095d95514b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(-5x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398191} {:answer/id #uuid "76a9807d-df1a-5832-b429-0890d06c222f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5sin⁡(-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76a9807d-df1a-5832-b429-0890d06c222f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5sin⁡(-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398192} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aeaedd6-8135-5962-8437-2a69c460095e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e694bf71-afc9-51ff-82ce-ddde47237115" :question/math-academy-id "q-118442" :question/problem "![](images/99/997eb5cc08dcff78cb32f5b47b5d066546854b05f3b066953772e456e713d773.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could be used to describe the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/1e/1e532c806d81c3c4015d78f814dc4ea3fc3483f22d34ade385894df2e8e7e3c5.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis.\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $5.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=5sin⁡(-x).$\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n5sin⁡(-x)=-5sin⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-5sin⁡x.$ However, $y=-5sin⁡x$ is not among the given options."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186398193 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059764 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "85090bdf-4f1b-5f79-a751-48521ad98895" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection of a Sine or Cosine Curve Over the Vertical Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62375a92-edd9-5873-9edf-ef0ddd9dccaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6sin⁡(-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398195} {:answer/id #uuid "1f1a872d-3b88-5a7f-8b75-ab249c2d63b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(-x)+6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398196} {:answer/id #uuid "839a4786-7b8b-587f-884b-34a3e1fb530a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(-\\frac{x}{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398197} {:answer/id #uuid "fbef0156-6d81-5b0b-80b2-e4c086df567d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}sin⁡(-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398198} {:answer/id #uuid "ab0aa795-c7d5-534e-922f-0ead75cd6cdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(-6x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398199}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62375a92-edd9-5873-9edf-ef0ddd9dccaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=6sin⁡(-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398195} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f0f56dd-8c86-5cbb-8889-d10079bd9452" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186398194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ef71e5dd-2133-5139-bcc4-313a9e867ac5" :question/math-academy-id "q-118443" :question/problem "![](images/f0/f012ee5880b4ec6c4c2cad4aa44da672b789f21c7f9969dcec50a25596e47c4b.png)\n\nWhich of the following equations could be used to describe the graph shown above?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/bc/bc61bf27fb210e0dc849829d5c287936fcb3f73bc279e98af8a64ba4085b8309.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis.\n\n- Finally, stretch it parallel to the $y$-axis by a stretch factor of $6.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=6sin⁡(-x).$\n\nNotice that, because the sine function is odd, the $y$-axis reflection has the same effect as the $x$-axis reflection, i.e.,\n\n$$\n6sin⁡(-x)=-6sin⁡x.\n$$\n\nSo the graph could also be viewed as $y=-6sin⁡x.$ However, $y=-6sin⁡x$ is not among the given options."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253480 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059765 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fe335eb-dc4a-5ea0-83cf-ad929e19b908" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Reflection and Shifting of a Sine or Cosine Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31777cec-aa4a-531c-8f91-1af7a0f66a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-cos⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250973} {:answer/id #uuid "3c737870-c937-5be1-9306-cc57cf97a411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251056} {:answer/id #uuid "4c303ce3-dff7-58a3-b70c-6bed684870f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-cos⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251183} {:answer/id #uuid "b4c0f728-a1b5-55fd-890b-dbd31577858c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=sin⁡(1-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252145} {:answer/id #uuid "e3100428-23e3-5316-a71c-cc8576b92026" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡(1-x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c737870-c937-5be1-9306-cc57cf97a411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-sin⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251056} :answer-field/id #uuid "011a22c2-f8f2-5ad2-9c5f-c7e5ef1ebd65" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1484f94-afd5-5ba7-9351-7b1e33dca84c" :question/math-academy-id "q-118450" :question/problem "![](images/98/98e3dc2a664f5096d2b7c9ba7bf7fc56faa189cd573a3de462e0b249fbc90af6.png)\n\nIdentify the equation that generated the graph above." :question/worked-solution "![](images/84/8499856915baa0bf144561dd35143e024586f4d417ac4f9c1ee48d7586e0f26c.png)\n\nTo plot the given curve, we follow these steps:\n\n- Start with the graph of $y=sin⁡x.$\n\n- Then, reflect it in the $x$-axis to give $y=-sin⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, translate it by $1$ unit **down** to give $y=-sin⁡x-1.$\n\nTherefore, the given curve is the graph of $y=-sin⁡x-1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253482 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61861939-69d4-5b1e-a0f0-6a1b383b775b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ed39256-683e-5ef6-a893-2ffd1ac7cfd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250952} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcffef7-c4e0-5618-a54c-3d0b1bd5feba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251053} {:answer/id #uuid "5a24d0d7-0cee-5b3c-8d59-814969cbbcad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251294} {:answer/id #uuid "b9016ea9-a887-53a4-97d6-743534d4d445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252181} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a06d70-c6d7-5b07-b5fa-a43959fc8051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b9016ea9-a887-53a4-97d6-743534d4d445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252181} :answer-field/id #uuid "736e577a-1579-5c66-a73e-dca1bc5214d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "054b33c1-48d0-5971-9cd6-626efbf760f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-11854" :question/problem "![](images/af/af896b84fd035bc81e97ab6214858ec7627c12b7b9ff25f18483839f4e389ba7.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of a left-opening parabola is the right-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/54/5410d736f7bae2f5c968d52083745b91279913045405ad75d154cf6b05bba4bd.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(2,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096711 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "61861939-69d4-5b1e-a0f0-6a1b383b775b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Horizontal Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e81d39f-516e-5368-bc2d-95dfd56ecc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250951} {:answer/id #uuid "5c03ac82-c231-5272-a9f5-a8427c78969f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251313} {:answer/id #uuid "689bd0b0-919d-55db-882e-177fbc8a49bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251412} {:answer/id #uuid "9087b924-4289-55bc-b40d-7d3076a7e6b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251783} {:answer/id #uuid "e93901db-4d3a-50ca-a596-af6453f3997b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2e81d39f-516e-5368-bc2d-95dfd56ecc89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-6,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250951} :answer-field/id #uuid "1177d6ca-055d-590d-ba84-4ee5a4872460" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252840}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4afc9701-6ad5-5e60-8fef-e96b8a6fe757" :question/math-academy-id "q-11858" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b1d78038032973d7bb5738b67963e0bfadb258e4c1eb06f191496bded406696.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of a right-opening parabola is the left-most point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/aa/aaf295d69a75c976df8aaf02ee6680486c502f7ce5719b41682cdf2499cab984.png)\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(-6,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253494 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083344 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2796c11a-4fa6-505d-9e0a-aa25efdb9c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250892} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec32c39-76e1-531c-a980-52d6069a233f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251335} {:answer/id #uuid "946eb912-f177-51c6-8055-52cb6bf80ad7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251830} {:answer/id #uuid "9c077374-79f0-523e-825a-f5521b2be909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251898} {:answer/id #uuid "bd295589-d9fe-5cee-b9fd-e8e6c502b237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252223}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ec32c39-76e1-531c-a980-52d6069a233f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251335} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8899cbd-a6dc-541a-a768-564111ff4288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253151}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bfa67a4d-773b-5a56-87ac-2e520036ce73" :question/math-academy-id "q-118618" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u={x}^{2}+1.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u={x}^{2}+1$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{({x}^{2}+1)^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+1}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{u}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-3/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[-2{u}^{-1/2}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}-1) \\\\  & =-(0-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083496 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083344 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6dc2621a-77e1-56d9-af63-1917eaa933b2" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Improper Integrals With an Unbounded Upper Limit Using Substitution"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5844549f-b897-5a58-bf08-acfbe8efa2a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251276} {:answer/id #uuid "7667e2f4-ee83-5482-af7c-d2bdd84fc202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251526} {:answer/id #uuid "a218e653-d88f-5440-be12-f0ad6810fe89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251949} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cc8e9f-2139-5926-aacc-b95747012288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252321} {:answer/id #uuid "f79ecc31-c3ea-5c7d-ae4d-ea007b99bc9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7667e2f4-ee83-5482-af7c-d2bdd84fc202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251526} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f22f9c0-c715-5c9f-b358-40ff3aa9350f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253084}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8d988115-5b28-5cc4-948a-8ebc728e32c9" :question/math-academy-id "q-118619" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we substitute $u=1+{x}^{2}.$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\text{d}u=x\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSince $u\\to \\infty $ when $x\\to \\infty ,$ the table for the limits of integration using the rule $u=1+{x}^{2}$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $\\infty $ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $1$ | $\\infty $ |\n\nSo, the integral in terms of the new variable $u$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we proceed by setting the upper bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to \\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(1+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{1}^{a}{u}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }[-{u}^{-1}]_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\frac{1}{u}]}_{1}^{a} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\frac{1}{a}-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(0-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386882 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115409 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f7e817a0-edcf-54ca-ba4c-ffe91af04675" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Height of a Triangle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b161b259-2a2c-531b-b051-34dd949dc4d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/16c9990277df1c1b212ff154c4951b59301ef5b7f8360f82b041e73a5a3a984b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386884} {:answer/id #uuid "e0792fd2-fe4a-5351-bd8d-d02531595632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/10/10e9f5a6af4d6d2b9c6392de91432b09620107d3dc645fc6ca65fdbda52f7c83.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386885} {:answer/id #uuid "b85c7ea1-4023-5d0d-86b4-0459134b26d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/ce3493e78660cfa84515970053105b95df5542eafbc4630c1e5b0afe9f658036.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386886} {:answer/id #uuid "6c9daab7-90f4-5583-88a7-85210ed3b622" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/16/168ba7b705d9a579dc11408cc7bf60fb13249c5d4c6a25c3c490c09cfc77ec38.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386887} {:answer/id #uuid "26d31697-89a3-57f9-9d21-e473be574ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/301c31fa98ce5c016337f422566b94a742e3da126b76f9060e4b13d6a8e86358.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386888}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "26d31697-89a3-57f9-9d21-e473be574ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/30/301c31fa98ce5c016337f422566b94a742e3da126b76f9060e4b13d6a8e86358.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386888} :answer-field/id #uuid "05bed876-047b-5aad-854a-94fee100e605" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386883}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3f1782d4-ceb0-5897-9f90-478b4c1242ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-118692" :question/problem "![](images/55/5548481ccbe3aee88fd4b346ad53777b7101223c19f7e8408c8ef85bb0d9df25.png)\n\nGiven the right triangle $△PQR$ shown above, which of the following diagrams represents the height corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}?$" :question/worked-solution "The height (or altitude) corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PR}$ is a line segment that \n\n- runs from the opposite vertex $Q$ to the line $\\overset{⟷}{PR},$ and\n\n- is perpendicular to $\\overset{⟷}{PR}.$\n\nThe correct diagram is shown below.\n\n![](images/30/301c31fa98ce5c016337f422566b94a742e3da126b76f9060e4b13d6a8e86358.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253501 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105922 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e93fd7f-070f-5359-aabb-cba5cc275015" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Convergent or Divergent Integrals With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "256aa671-a669-57d4-b91d-ced24db81e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250876} {:answer/id #uuid "5a277d68-b884-5e7d-b52b-01b0500937b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251295} {:answer/id #uuid "ac672eaa-7a45-596b-aff7-c0a0076f4abb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252053} {:answer/id #uuid "c6240e34-c165-5a5d-b60f-e16d15bc4bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252304} {:answer/id #uuid "eb6959c8-58ee-530d-bb52-f3078500cbf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252618}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "256aa671-a669-57d4-b91d-ced24db81e43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250876} :answer-field/id #uuid "92e60e35-c089-5683-b48c-03659e05f5af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "260fa3cd-f59d-50c6-a56e-8cfbd29393f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-118755" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is convergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent\n- ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x$ is divergent" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an improper integral in which both the left and right endpoints are unbounded:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nIf the integrand $f(x)$ is continuous on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ then we can break the integral up into two improper integrals, each with a single unbounded endpoint:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nThen, we can compute each of the two integrals separately and combine their results. \n\n**Watch out!** If either of the two integrals on the right-hand side is divergent, then the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution.  Let $u={x}^{2}+4$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4)+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's evaluate each integral in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{a}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{0}^{a} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to \\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to \\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the required integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+4))|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{2}-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs $a\\to -\\infty $, we have that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({a}^{2}+4)\\to \\infty .$ So the integral diverges.\n\n- Statement III is true. The integral in statement III can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nHowever, both integrals on the right-hand side are divergent. Therefore, the integral on the left-hand side is also divergent.\n\nIn conclusion,  only statements II and III are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253503 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115336 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a916099-a237-5612-b7f1-3679b7ff069d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37f6e98b-9e74-5362-9415-76b0e5788bd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{26}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251016} {:answer/id #uuid "412b51d2-1c6c-5e96-b7f4-3a46ab2a6b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251093} {:answer/id #uuid "5df0c213-f26e-5d44-b086-a8082f8ff356" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{22}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251329} {:answer/id #uuid "6a726442-67f6-5b29-aa0d-827d6fcd828e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{18}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251427} {:answer/id #uuid "b7d0d4d2-0d27-5b44-a286-f8c17048fc8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{32}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "412b51d2-1c6c-5e96-b7f4-3a46ab2a6b3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{28}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251093} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd987d2b-51a1-5e48-9b29-2cadc9f301dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253251}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "574c517e-3266-5464-9df0-9e740c923895" :question/math-academy-id "q-118757" :question/problem "![](images/95/95b534f8c159b37884e32b730d9aa502565ff4971cd7d08903ffbfaa94b76623.png)\n\nConsider $△PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{―}{RH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{―}{PQ}.$ If $m∠PQR={118}^{∘},$ calculate $m∠QRH.$" :question/worked-solution "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠HQR+m∠PQR & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR+{118}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠HQR & ={62}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $△QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{∘},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m∠QRH+m∠HQR+m∠RHQ & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{62}^{∘}+{90}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH+{152}^{∘} & ={180}^{∘} \\\\ m∠QRH & ={28}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253505 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105923 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3fe1f468-9e8d-58fc-bd61-cfe1191a5a19" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Improper Integral With Unbounded Limits"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f1a364e-3b14-5b1e-925a-17619c3c4554" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250954} {:answer/id #uuid "425e34a3-6e0d-5c26-9432-07697c0aa0e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251107} {:answer/id #uuid "a31e745b-dd53-5147-a790-8537255c9700" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "The integral diverges" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251958} {:answer/id #uuid "a6581fa3-e9c8-5704-aa7c-c199f43ad4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251988} {:answer/id #uuid "fae68304-e087-5c8c-9cc4-675e9c2b99ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6581fa3-e9c8-5704-aa7c-c199f43ad4a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251988} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4fec40e-2040-58e3-9660-c87c436962eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253237}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c8456d00-1e90-5830-966e-53837304687a" :question/math-academy-id "q-118774" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{e}^{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-{e}^{-u}+C \\\\  & =-{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-{e}^{-{x}^{2}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(-{e}^{0}-(-{e}^{-{a}^{2}})) \\\\  & =-1+0 \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{e}^{{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-1+1 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253509 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096761 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d27b85e4-ddae-5b5f-965a-5dff51b8449b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Vertex of a Vertical Parabola Given Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "270439e3-e2ab-5a45-87bf-95f9becc4e56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-8,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250889} {:answer/id #uuid "463b87ff-48d0-5886-a575-321db25afa2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(8,4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251134} {:answer/id #uuid "68421f23-fdb5-513b-aad2-454ec11b2707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251407} {:answer/id #uuid "6c15702c-4ffc-5fc6-bfbe-fc6f04ea6b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251437} {:answer/id #uuid "88b53079-b103-5a08-a70c-3440fd7a01bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251705}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68421f23-fdb5-513b-aad2-454ec11b2707" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251407} :answer-field/id #uuid "835c1580-a670-5c3e-b1f5-64dbfd79c4e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "acdf7c4b-4f3a-5142-ba6b-26da51bdc76a" :question/math-academy-id "q-11878" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2b78b376173589b18d5e8da73897d9552f41a00aab523c0e76892623b586eb53.png)\n\nWhat is the vertex of the parabola shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The vertex of a downward-opening parabola is the highest point of the parabola.\n\n![](images/3c/3c547f893dc1cf2e71c17022ea7c81034214d7aedabe78bf719ebd44b96da329.png)\n\n \n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the vertex are $(4,8).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166813 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "443c0bd0-2e77-50a8-91d3-a288d4ee852b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Order of an Ordinary Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18ed852c-a746-5e34-8210-a1c6c19157ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250771} {:answer/id #uuid "36e85211-b2b9-5649-bb4b-17bb47e95e41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251005} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9ca850-3368-5c8e-a5b8-495183e07f87" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251590} {:answer/id #uuid "9e263215-e831-5e53-a204-bd8750fe03b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251918} {:answer/id #uuid "a7d903f5-c111-595f-8377-514289f9d574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18ed852c-a746-5e34-8210-a1c6c19157ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250771} :answer-field/id #uuid "0050ba89-30e8-5e34-8905-20291c552790" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252802}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "345cfcae-2112-5c63-808e-4798d18f1830" :question/math-academy-id "q-118781" :question/problem "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-{y}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}}={y}^{5}?$" :question/worked-solution "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{4}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{4}},$ which has order $4.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253529 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096615 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5856f3cc-5423-5a97-b2fd-7f44e0797ec7" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Logarithmic Function With a General Base"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010fae26-d218-5d85-80be-b5da7fa03138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{xln⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250562} {:answer/id #uuid "1d7274d3-9d6c-566f-8ab0-5d6dadc206db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250801} {:answer/id #uuid "3fea77d7-dd38-54a3-ab38-de4a5e6af670" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{xln⁡5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251082} {:answer/id #uuid "90d31982-431c-5c96-9fd1-28cbe6930c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{xln⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251788 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409265}]} {:answer/id #uuid "e56177c0-fb1c-54a8-8c75-934ef63a1c08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90d31982-431c-5c96-9fd1-28cbe6930c37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{xln⁡4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251788} :answer-field/id #uuid "35ef16c9-7f8c-57e6-86fd-b4dbee5a6bfa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252894}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b9de855f-8e10-5bff-89a6-c3649ce380b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-11900" :question/problem "Given that $y=5{log}_{4}⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "To differentiate a logarithm with base $a,$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{a}⁡x)=\\frac{1}{xln⁡a}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{log}_{4}⁡x) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({log}_{4}⁡x) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{xln⁡4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{xln⁡4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096696 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f570287d-b0e4-53bb-8fa1-37348ca9202f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Differential Equation Using Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73b6602d-f037-52b0-8c48-d0885dedafc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=2(x+9{e}^{-3x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399991} {:answer/id #uuid "30b063ed-08ab-5694-9771-9427e9a07c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=x-2{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399992} {:answer/id #uuid "95477359-7c43-51b4-a1ff-890600150acd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=x+{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399993} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2d9c2f-bf87-54d1-a5c5-0103894f75e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=x-18{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399994} {:answer/id #uuid "3694a7a7-cd54-5712-bdcb-421abc079139" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=2(x-3{e}^{-3x})+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30b063ed-08ab-5694-9771-9427e9a07c6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f(x)=x-2{e}^{-3x}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399992} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f36605a-43ff-547f-a708-ab078c73b400" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399990}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8e422fe9-fc97-5df0-b6a5-ce1fd6f9f5cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-119037" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation  ${f}^{′}(x)-6{e}^{-3x}=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate the derivative term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =1+6{e}^{-3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides of the equation with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+6{e}^{-3x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\text{d}x+6\\int {e}^{-3x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x+6(-\\frac{1}{3}{e}^{-3x}) \\\\  & =x-2{e}^{-3x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253537 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096697 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "192bb38b-b609-56ff-b560-becfc38ea070" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Solution of a Second Order Differential Equation by Integrating Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23cc8ac7-5e09-54ce-b6d3-e159d67ab8ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{4}+4{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250861} {:answer/id #uuid "5901205a-e2f9-52f3-b94e-a09bbbbe0521" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{4}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251285} {:answer/id #uuid "60767b47-bf28-5927-9170-56b9e257c34c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{4}-5{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251342} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e1b67-45cb-51ac-a61f-ee18bbb149b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252358} {:answer/id #uuid "f4155827-e220-55c0-a8b5-d48c90df5a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-4{x}^{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc4e1b67-45cb-51ac-a61f-ee18bbb149b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252358} :answer-field/id #uuid "aeb1d879-87da-5436-bd2a-6290c0abb0e6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253110}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c5323a95-ccb4-5698-8e94-ec2b6a694be3" :question/math-academy-id "q-119043" :question/problem "Solve the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+5=6{x}^{2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}=6{x}^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nNow, integrating the equation once, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (6{x}^{2}-5)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int \\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =6(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3})-5x+C \\\\  & =2{x}^{3}-5x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating a second time, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2{x}^{3}-5x+C)\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x-5\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+C\\int \\text{d}x \\\\  & =2(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-5(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})+Cx+D \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}-\\frac{5{x}^{2}}{2}+Cx+D.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that because we had a second-order equation, our general solution contains *two* arbitrary constants, $C$ and $D.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253545 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096699 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4ab07a7-d1ed-5f44-8915-09e7b2418825" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Solution of a Differential Equation With a Given Condition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4861751d-591d-55c3-986e-ef2a5b44047f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=x-\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2}+\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251157} {:answer/id #uuid "66412d8c-05ca-5d19-b457-71e9ce3cbc83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251394} {:answer/id #uuid "73dd9002-7420-57dd-aa1f-4e2c936b2b76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2x+{e}^{x/2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251508} {:answer/id #uuid "906cd958-b4d4-5e47-8a8a-6773bb3989db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251780} {:answer/id #uuid "f2723fb9-3ffe-529a-aa32-10cf5b96b2dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+\\frac{{e}^{x/2}}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "906cd958-b4d4-5e47-8a8a-6773bb3989db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251780} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2e489f5-5c3c-511b-8803-0c93ae9edf09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253116}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d5aa7094-d36f-5005-a0c6-3e11c04ef4b9" :question/math-academy-id "q-119083" :question/problem "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}-2x={e}^{x/2}$ that satisfies the condition $y(0)=2.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to give\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=2x+{e}^{x/2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x,$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+{e}^{x/2})\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =2\\int x\\,\\text{d}x+\\int {e}^{x/2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2{e}^{x/2}+C \\\\  & ={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo our general solution is $y={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}+C.$ To find a particular solution, we substitute the condition $y(0)=2$ into our solution and obtain a value for $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & ={0}^{2}+2{e}^{0}+C \\\\ 2 & =0+2(1)+C \\\\ C & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\ny={x}^{2}+2{e}^{x/2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096614 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce3c28-857b-5538-b362-cf52526e3688" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum Containing a Natural Logarithm Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "26bf5cf8-eaf1-5b10-8d2a-662c31b3b508" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250885} {:answer/id #uuid "783b0f4c-199c-5928-b08e-dcb4df838be4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{2}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251549} {:answer/id #uuid "89f1e417-01f6-5b82-ba77-84fd142edcc2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251714} {:answer/id #uuid "e0afa8a6-a60b-5d30-b8a6-b2c64b3b9e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252532} {:answer/id #uuid "fe2d11d8-637f-5d1f-abb2-68fd4be88574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252782 :task-item/_responses [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186409245}]}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe2d11d8-637f-5d1f-abb2-68fd4be88574" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+\\frac{1}{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252782} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef06db64-440f-52c2-ab98-0824b44d15d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2a7a5557-d240-5298-a285-f0853a897b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-11911" :question/problem "Given that $y=2x+ln⁡x$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2x+ln⁡x) \\\\  & =2+\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23789a74-f1e3-5e26-8678-21f84a11a12b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ad94dc2-96d1-5f82-a763-87dd2729f0de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/78/7832a2110292cb364cf06c24eb51e9bb1ee42fe430ac8fcc381af047a09125e4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250651} {:answer/id #uuid "104071b2-3939-5957-8b0b-115c9954484c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/ce/cece096a5b267821994e9b6d8142f0d7ea684bbe8e28da7cdb01b87a73f1db4c.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250695} {:answer/id #uuid "37599f90-3244-5785-b02a-3b8c6636ce9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/96/96359fe35288b866cbf9fd929e2515af04a4ec0dc565a4cd4af9d3a8fcd20f94.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251006} {:answer/id #uuid "56cfa623-a5a2-5b84-a1b7-c443f45bdc6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d7ecaa30c600519d6bfcc7a43a4275a55d6f6cc1b52b48c8e7b117e7a772343e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251263} {:answer/id #uuid "a29f2347-9127-52fe-8d0d-29d70ef13664" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/8e/8ede6d26a62570f4037ecc58c377e95389ab00dbee36b60c46e6fb2021e01cea.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56cfa623-a5a2-5b84-a1b7-c443f45bdc6e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d7/d7ecaa30c600519d6bfcc7a43a4275a55d6f6cc1b52b48c8e7b117e7a772343e.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251263} :answer-field/id #uuid "56370ecd-d3f8-58ef-b871-fb4e753f3b83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252955}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0b17887e-edc7-5942-b62d-5bad58805bd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-119132" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{{e}^{-x/4}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{-x/4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-4{e}^{-x/4}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-x/4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=0,3,6.$\n\n![](images/a4/a4b534bd33daabc94aa3c86935b1fd4c0be723fafbc418ed382716541a864293.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386903 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23789a74-f1e3-5e26-8678-21f84a11a12b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdbca57f-16aa-507a-9243-fc5de6668bda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/4a/4acfee397d2c301aa7d005c8467319969bdfe1a487c284e51024a1851ef8a2e3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386905} {:answer/id #uuid "0e615e5e-edb5-5cf5-9379-57b9b7c05caa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0e/0e2d84423e017f6d126ef6b997a50d62a7de30f91c24aa64f2525ce4ae33cdd3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386906} {:answer/id #uuid "da6e7a98-d892-56d4-877b-036894b8ae90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/146aa5565c845f3fbfa66e6c215224236869199844cdbe51f2671c781b1c3705.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386907} {:answer/id #uuid "35676c31-bad6-527d-8d41-0491046fbe5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e93190f38d95bd30749fbd1342812c93f593a1d78243f810a3822d5d30eff82.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386908} {:answer/id #uuid "c70ce449-3435-5f49-bbc8-8c5d5d34005e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/55/557708220ba3876b0ae9dbf1d265d11e8d98cf1102ad084d93419543ede35c4b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386909}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da6e7a98-d892-56d4-877b-036894b8ae90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/146aa5565c845f3fbfa66e6c215224236869199844cdbe51f2671c781b1c3705.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386907} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dd2a4ff-6ada-5a27-9174-b3c1a697aa04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386904}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06acaa38-08e2-563c-a31d-7a927dbf62a2" :question/math-academy-id "q-119139" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-4{e}^{2x}?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-4{e}^{2x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-4\\int {e}^{2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/cc/cc2ee25a85be3ffdb055853f7665bd53d34003a74b96a14bb947bed39b18e83f.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386910 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "23789a74-f1e3-5e26-8678-21f84a11a12b" :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Solution Curves of a Differential Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "641c6f0c-3eaf-561b-8356-41cd00f60ef4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/d5/d5f4f2b65878b9c152b5c87e1fac0473f0e76455fc5762ba10f21555d68a60c4.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386912} {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f2778-f343-5bd4-8b1f-ffa35692fd8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e475d534a8f090caa9611d865e70a9717969ddc541c55d98200e19031bffe84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386913} {:answer/id #uuid "21fe63ba-ca2a-5062-b8c9-c489ae9d0da5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/a6/a65cbd746c3bb9abe495af168fd7c63e83ecf3d942c5fb478f3d7d312d140b4d.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386914} {:answer/id #uuid "993af450-e9a5-57fb-a473-cf3bc2a14c65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/95/95b3d8fc43562341f93a2bccbcbe36d2a070a2dd6687606175ab5e9580f86e2f.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386915} {:answer/id #uuid "6f16a9da-248f-5eb6-9d9c-67a9d932876b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0d/0d14e369d621a6c2d06b4db1485ff43f6af4be2014e9d26564c9ca11e9bd38cd.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386916}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dc9f2778-f343-5bd4-8b1f-ffa35692fd8d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/3e/3e475d534a8f090caa9611d865e70a9717969ddc541c55d98200e19031bffe84.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386913} :answer-field/id #uuid "33aa3810-dbfd-5996-9796-56ae579de721" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a05048db-be50-5d0c-8e66-1eb6eda34260" :question/math-academy-id "q-119142" :question/problem "Which of the following shows some of the solution curves of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\frac{3(x-1)^{2}}{4}?$" :question/worked-solution "Integrating the equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (-\\frac{3(x-1)^{2}}{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ y & =-\\frac{3}{4}\\int (x-1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}(\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(x-1)^{3}}{4}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now plot the solution curves by selecting different values for $C.$ The sketch below shows the solution curves for $C=-2,0,2.$\n\n![](images/e0/e0ff2e64925dcd8bdf298c47815f6e2982fe6091e1ff5dea042f585c67ef1721.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192836 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a8bf54-5523-5eaa-a236-b8931d1c3dba" :knowledge-point/title "Improper Integrals Involving Arctangent Using the Second Result"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "778b9605-56f5-5801-9b37-1afd366faf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251540} {:answer/id #uuid "87ad96d2-dc83-5457-9f3f-9e32968fedd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251698} {:answer/id #uuid "89faada8-0f27-5ebf-91ff-42f76e4798d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251715} {:answer/id #uuid "8fbcadc7-9fad-5324-82df-926b4ba0aa6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251774} {:answer/id #uuid "926f3fa9-4b12-5f2a-a81c-eccdefd843ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251807}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87ad96d2-dc83-5457-9f3f-9e32968fedd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251698} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f340d0e-66a6-54c5-a2ae-f77be864712f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "72e807f7-837a-5836-9313-38f63281c554" :question/math-academy-id "q-119199" :question/problem "${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following result:\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{1}{{k}^{2}+{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{k})+C\n$$\n\nWe proceed by setting the lower bound equal to some parameter $a,$ integrating as usual, and then taking the limit as $a\\to -\\infty .$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{[\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{u}{\\sqrt{3}})]}_{a}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}})-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\frac{\\pi }{6}-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the end behavior of the function $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}}),$ shown in the graph below:\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=-\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{3}})]=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n![](images/11/11c74897fe914599a78c5997767bad83d13ddfb1e35ee66622d8880743688bad.png)\n\nUsing this to evaluate our integral, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{1}\\frac{1}{{u}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}u & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot {lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{a}{\\sqrt{3}})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6\\sqrt{3}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3\\sqrt{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253566 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "38b450bd-b43a-507c-9bbe-0aaf7a64a3ed" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Point That Lies on a Transformed Curve Given Another Transformed Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42a70a80-9905-5343-a34c-136a375e25ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251108} {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c8745-888b-5fc6-9d1a-fc1686129698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} {:answer/id #uuid "a8ce837a-8964-565e-9e2d-f825bac56dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252014} {:answer/id #uuid "d6b4c14b-cbd8-5494-b7e3-3692ec97eb35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252455} {:answer/id #uuid "e0fa8370-7c51-5755-8595-d094b48e3a78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252534}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d7c8745-888b-5fc6-9d1a-fc1686129698" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251913} :answer-field/id #uuid "2019c876-575f-5be0-a410-079acd66193f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4d38949b-9d50-5e0f-bb63-ea8ff36967c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-119308" :question/problem "The point $(2,3)$ lies on the curve $y=f(x)-1.$ Which of the following points **must** lie on $y=-f(x)+4?$" :question/worked-solution "**Method 1**\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x)+4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- First, take the graph $y=f(x)-1.$\n\n- Then, we shift the graph vertically by $1$ unit up and get the graph of $y=f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,3)$ will be mapped to the point\n\n$$\n(2,3+1)=(2,4).\n$$\n\n- Next, we reflect the result across the $x$-axis and get the graph of $y=-f(x).$ After this transformation, the point $(2,4)$ will be mapped  to the point\n\n$$\n(2,-(4))=(2,-4).\n$$\n\n- Finally, shift the result vertically by $4$ units up and obtain the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ After this transformation, the point $(2,-4)$ will be mapped to the point \n\n$$\n(2,-4+4)=(2,0).\n$$\n\n**Method 2**\n\nSince the point $(2,3)$ lies on the graph of $y=f(x)-1$, we have that \n\n$$\nf(2)-1=3\\,⟹\\,f(2)=4.\n$$\n\nNow consider the graph of $y=-f(x)+4.$ Substituting $x=2$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-f(2)+4 \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the point $(2,0)$ must lie on $y=-f(x)+4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253574 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059641 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3332b534-bd04-5e86-995e-1e6d691d8e1b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1815e8ee-c289-5f64-9f4d-5df43b4aab39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250764} {:answer/id #uuid "25423e87-2e31-52ea-9bed-9680a8ad4d75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250872} {:answer/id #uuid "380b2f49-3850-505e-a125-48611fc7669a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251019} {:answer/id #uuid "b6fc9b1c-0a28-54c2-82c9-b8ff1ad7eae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252166} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0ddaa2-dc14-5f83-8293-f70b43e52381" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252200}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "380b2f49-3850-505e-a125-48611fc7669a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251019} :answer-field/id #uuid "a308e80e-fc90-57c9-9815-5d63c4e60f59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e264613-6b71-5c6c-b1c0-6da9257b1f1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-119371" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression ${sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the expression:\n\n$$\n{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, we use ${sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta =1$ to simplify the expression in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253577 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "868125f0-22e0-57fc-9620-f3242419a689" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Rational Expression Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08ed664b-fff1-5712-ac2e-5525f9cdacef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250634} {:answer/id #uuid "3b69e247-6d23-5ff1-a976-eb6503b80dcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta +1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251045} {:answer/id #uuid "58c39cbe-5a9b-575c-ae8a-d7f92d34e4dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251282} {:answer/id #uuid "923c3e09-f2e0-5124-82ce-83280737e8aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251804} {:answer/id #uuid "b061ebd1-c842-5f84-80fa-eec468f6b43d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "08ed664b-fff1-5712-ac2e-5525f9cdacef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250634} :answer-field/id #uuid "89a2bbe3-2ba2-5a56-9f75-7bcd2efd08bd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253041}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e604df29-4056-5a18-af72-ee9756feedd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-119387" :question/problem "$\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot ({sin}^{2}⁡\\theta +{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta )} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta \\cdot 1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\cancel{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{{sin}^{5}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{3}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253578 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059642 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "868125f0-22e0-57fc-9620-f3242419a689" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Rational Expression Using the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103f5b2e-5c31-5097-a363-9f6afaafdbb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250694} {:answer/id #uuid "4b13d84e-fa74-5321-92fd-9d3ab8999d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251178} {:answer/id #uuid "53ddef33-f78b-53ce-9fde-d6f939e3fb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251239} {:answer/id #uuid "a784c34d-c4aa-591d-a102-942798280f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{sec}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251996} {:answer/id #uuid "da6b22ed-a42d-50cf-92ea-c1fdfd820128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+\\operatorname{csc}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252472}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53ddef33-f78b-53ce-9fde-d6f939e3fb9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251239} :answer-field/id #uuid "f15dbfd9-4666-5a0a-a3af-8408df3d5a73" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253227}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c41ec619-a5e7-5d9c-af90-cb7a886021e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-119392" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ from the first two terms of the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the Pythagorean identity to rewrite the expression in the parentheses, and then we simplify the resulting expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot 1+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+{(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x{sin}^{3}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{3}⁡x+1}{{sin}^{2}⁡x}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+{csc}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253579 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124512 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e73b8bcf-79c2-5326-b396-d7d3e06badaa" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Standard Matrix of a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17680857-523a-56e1-8420-c08210489de4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250760} {:answer/id #uuid "1b2349f9-546b-554c-b41a-858b240c556b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250785} {:answer/id #uuid "63dd99ab-fead-531a-9761-7733e0bb2f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251368} {:answer/id #uuid "87876316-f8ab-5e4e-adf4-44606bad247e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251693} {:answer/id #uuid "dd4a9ee1-81a3-52f3-91aa-ac10ad95cfc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252503}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63dd99ab-fead-531a-9761-7733e0bb2f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251368} :answer-field/id #uuid "7c78b755-d795-5ba9-92eb-1cb1d5ff8158" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253020}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0145758b-88e9-57c3-9578-3a41888f61e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-11945" :question/problem "Find the standard matrix that  represents  the  linear  transformation $T$, where\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We require a $2\\times 2$ matrix $T$ such that\n    \n\n$$\nT\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot x+3\\cdot y \\\\ 0\\cdot x+1\\cdot y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNotice that\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2x+3y \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n    Therefore, the matrix which represents the linear transformation $T$ is\n    \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253581 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059666 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33c614c8-e574-54c4-afe7-ea7cbe93a089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250984} {:answer/id #uuid "82138ae6-184b-5b43-9d31-1dafcb47b464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251649} {:answer/id #uuid "866785b4-4b0d-5ed0-ab10-df319b0d8757" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251680} {:answer/id #uuid "cb17904f-e9e2-5ce4-ac6b-159a7742e47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252351} {:answer/id #uuid "f85fd59f-f673-55d7-9727-39ba21278ce9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82138ae6-184b-5b43-9d31-1dafcb47b464" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251649} :answer-field/id #uuid "307233f6-b9b3-54e3-9937-9c7b41e5ed35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9b9e46d2-105c-587e-ad59-8da071cc7eea" :question/math-academy-id "q-119479" :question/problem "Find the exact value of  $\\operatorname{cos}⁡{195}^{∘}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${195}^{∘}={135}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={135}^{∘}$ and $y={60}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({195}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡({135}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{135}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{60}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{135}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{60}^{∘} \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253582 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059821 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "046c3fff-e187-5d6d-817c-fcad26542595" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250586} {:answer/id #uuid "4206c502-18de-5733-a8cd-4790cc23f4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251105} {:answer/id #uuid "459effbf-a2af-5f96-b9e0-38a98c849e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251129} {:answer/id #uuid "6c93157b-2ac8-5521-831f-507d005bc1c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251447} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8d63a7-52ee-5a72-956d-4feb52257b50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4206c502-18de-5733-a8cd-4790cc23f4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251105} :answer-field/id #uuid "af98a692-a950-5516-913c-d1527e2c4e36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253111}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24fefd74-9f51-579d-ab19-a20613b6de76" :question/math-academy-id "q-119482" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v+\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={30}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-{30}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡{30}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡{30}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253590 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096622 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af269509-92c0-5a82-b828-e8626f8e8eda" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Quotient Using Leibniz Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b89321c-e3ba-5c4f-81cd-a7cbf23d72d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{{(9-2x)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250653} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbd81fe-3865-51f1-85e7-89c40d3f7b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{{(9-2x)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250690} {:answer/id #uuid "3a22468e-a8b9-5374-a6b1-4af81ef69538" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4x-9}{{(9-2x)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251034} {:answer/id #uuid "bd9f93f8-6285-5333-9fbc-0e76b0bb68f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2x-9}{{(9-2x)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252231} {:answer/id #uuid "e86a868c-0137-5698-8032-3302efc36e18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4x-9}{{(9-2x)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fbd81fe-3865-51f1-85e7-89c40d3f7b84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{{(9-2x)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250690} :answer-field/id #uuid "05c220dd-0a63-5428-9d3d-76e098f2cb6c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4ae64d20-c5b4-50bb-94bc-ab5da04017e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-11969" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{-x}{9-2x}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $u=-x$ and $v=9-2x.$ Then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} & =-1\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quotient rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}-u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(9-2x)\\cdot (-1)-(-x)\\cdot (-2)}{{(9-2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-9+2x-2x}{{(9-2x)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{9}{{(9-2x)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186097026 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39a5acaf-0b97-5eec-b5dc-80f14127936e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251028} {:answer/id #uuid "5fc87138-62df-5eac-9d34-752a109148de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{x}{{(3x+5)}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251338} {:answer/id #uuid "794835b2-c8d2-5e60-99aa-d141cf6639aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251559} {:answer/id #uuid "80538af1-96e8-546c-8e04-53ab4e77cd46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2x}{{(3x+5)}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251634} {:answer/id #uuid "e543d792-894d-58ae-966d-434f788becaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5x}{{(3x+5)}^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252560}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "794835b2-c8d2-5e60-99aa-d141cf6639aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{30x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251559} :answer-field/id #uuid "dcc00428-34a0-5c80-bcd3-256eb4ce28ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253182}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4603accf-ccdb-5030-90b8-c9b568c4df0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-11973" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{3{x}^{2}}{(3x+5)^{2}}$, find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=3{x}^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)=(3x+5)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=6x,\\,{v}^{′}(x)=6(3x+5).\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $v.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(6x){(3x+5)}^{2}-(3{x}^{2})\\cdot 6(3x+5)}{{((3x+5)^{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6x(3x+5)(3x+5-3x)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6x(3x+5)(5)}{{(3x+5)}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{30x}{{(3x+5)}^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253599 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bc8c875-3393-5fd6-90cf-87737e16f2f2" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying a Trigonometric Expression Using a Common Denominator and the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55eb6103-d65c-564e-8146-1b697fcf6114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251257} {:answer/id #uuid "9c494cc4-f14a-523f-afab-b55ead4e2206" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251902} {:answer/id #uuid "9cabf4a9-d334-52aa-94a3-29c1226a338c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251905} {:answer/id #uuid "ac59de0c-94ce-555a-a9af-0f345fa9844c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252052} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfac5b4-b918-541c-bab0-02afa81cd836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252374}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac59de0c-94ce-555a-a9af-0f345fa9844c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252052} :answer-field/id #uuid "5889b846-4b45-54d4-a4c7-1acb0782d3ec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252962}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a4d16bb-7544-56af-8e64-b1dbb3f56d7c" :question/math-academy-id "q-119770" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$" :question/worked-solution "We put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the Pythagorean identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{({sin}^{2}⁡x+{cos}^{2}⁡x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096844 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "261910cd-a1a4-5c90-8caa-238208558458" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Derivative Using the Chain and Quotient Rules"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010ec989-6a70-5560-8c6d-96bcabcf07f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-x)^{2}}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250561} {:answer/id #uuid "185de746-f2a2-5bc0-a895-8eef91887f33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(x+1)}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250767} {:answer/id #uuid "5cf0aeab-facc-582b-9a39-b2c196aead5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251320} {:answer/id #uuid "c4f44c2b-b5c6-5adc-a37b-c3386048fc4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-x)}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252297} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b8be0a-88c7-5856-ade3-8586c358ec8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(1-2{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252547}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cf0aeab-facc-582b-9a39-b2c196aead5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{{({x}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251320} :answer-field/id #uuid "910e876b-71ca-514d-ad43-ceb4c9229334" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3000a138-321d-511f-bb2f-c25bd88c5e97" :question/math-academy-id "q-11978" :question/problem "Given that $y=\\frac{(x+1)^{2}}{{x}^{2}+1},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "We use the quotient rule with the numerator $u(x)=(x+1)^{2}$ and the denominator $v(x)={x}^{2}+1.$ \n\nDifferentiating $u(x)$ and $v(x),$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}(x)=2(x+1),\\,{v}^{′}(x)=2x.\n$$\n\nNote that we used the chain rule to differentiate $u.$\n\nNow, applying the quotient rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{u}^{′}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{′}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2(x+1))({x}^{2}+1)-((x+1)^{2})(2x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)-2x(x+1)^{2}}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(({x}^{2}+1)-x(x+1))}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)({x}^{2}+1-{x}^{2}-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)(1-x)}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1-{x}^{2})}{({x}^{2}+1)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253617 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059652 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b05f4be1-95d4-5137-bbaa-71c95862bb59" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions by Factoring an Alternate Form of the Pythagorean Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00677872-7616-5c6b-bc06-e85a41bca0b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250553} {:answer/id #uuid "60dff4f9-0626-5330-99dd-f03bb2eb6f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251345} {:answer/id #uuid "a43f7607-4390-56c0-ba26-6beaa6359216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251971} {:answer/id #uuid "bbc2c36b-5bb0-5f2e-8ad7-e904eb5d214b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252208} {:answer/id #uuid "bc732a44-7bfc-58d9-95fa-404ba29a3bd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bbc2c36b-5bb0-5f2e-8ad7-e904eb5d214b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252208} :answer-field/id #uuid "68996742-5572-5673-a555-2490c8c236c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a1b437a5-869a-5ccd-aeb4-884c52b20f38" :question/math-academy-id "q-119864" :question/problem "Simplify the expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the numerator using $1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}=\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor the numerator as a difference of squares, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1-{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1} & =\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{1} \\\\  & =-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x-1}=-(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253621 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110560 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35fe6748-f040-5318-84e0-f3f76c9c7250" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Cotangent-Cosecant Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27f0a8ce-4aec-512b-a809-fe80d7a37254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡z+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250896} {:answer/id #uuid "383e056a-7d1a-580d-8144-5ce601bf73a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{cot}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251020} {:answer/id #uuid "449a0196-c334-57cc-9c6b-f43d1c3715ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cot}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251123} {:answer/id #uuid "978b01e0-91fd-53a4-93a7-f12af31fb3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251854} {:answer/id #uuid "d552fd33-bdd6-5221-8186-0acd1c018978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{cot}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252441}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "978b01e0-91fd-53a4-93a7-f12af31fb3e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251854} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ef446b-20ba-56ee-b042-97aed74369c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253233}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d3d698d1-fddf-50d2-9b5a-51d1e576d1b5" :question/math-academy-id "q-119904" :question/problem "Simplify ${cos}^{2}⁡z\\,({cot}^{2}⁡z+1).$" :question/worked-solution "Using the identity $1+{cot}^{2}⁡z={csc}^{2}⁡z,$ we rewrite and simplify the expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡z\\,({cot}^{2}⁡z+1) & ={cos}^{2}⁡z\\cdot {csc}^{2}⁡z \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡z\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}⁡z\\cdot \\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡z} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡z}{{sin}^{2}⁡z} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z})}^{2} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}⁡z\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253627 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0abdcc32-cc35-5670-9c83-c4cbe912eee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250650} {:answer/id #uuid "57edb35e-9370-5374-9e06-ccc1975123a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251271} {:answer/id #uuid "882f41e4-7a0d-5ac6-8538-7f29f7c131ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251701} {:answer/id #uuid "d69eebe4-9936-53c3-8b7b-de492f9722de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252454} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3248bd-252a-5193-96ee-ea1bc9f698ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252795}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0abdcc32-cc35-5670-9c83-c4cbe912eee6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250650} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf9019c2-6685-5469-b58a-01ed2b00e34b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253164}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1a84df43-c63e-5997-bb62-0b42cedd5faa" :question/math-academy-id "q-120017" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nRewriting the given expression using the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253628 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "024f4b95-a6a5-5f2d-a751-2755ac5cce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250575} {:answer/id #uuid "0e20a623-1450-54d0-9595-8a7aa3109a42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250675} {:answer/id #uuid "3a0ea007-0dad-5df4-93e4-28082fff80d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251033} {:answer/id #uuid "8f9f6191-c575-5400-985e-09588bd93769" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251773} {:answer/id #uuid "adab3445-9088-5485-a515-0fc08abd7e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252067}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "adab3445-9088-5485-a515-0fc08abd7e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252067} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a4e8fb7-0cf4-569d-9a43-083899802b06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253044}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "11defe63-9b06-5fba-87b6-5807bf8b34d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120018" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the given expression by rewriting the $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ in the denominator, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253632 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059673 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a1adf34-b5ab-575a-b655-c479f3717df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250640} {:answer/id #uuid "199a6b8d-c1b5-52ec-8d2b-338601436874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250776} {:answer/id #uuid "38546a08-184f-5411-80ea-0b9a2a403009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251022} {:answer/id #uuid "65f52443-c286-5605-b6f6-ab3d3b725230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251392} {:answer/id #uuid "e2a2c78e-06dc-539e-b4e5-76443acce72b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+{cos}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a1adf34-b5ab-575a-b655-c479f3717df6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250640} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0d401dc-d0fa-51c1-b8e5-f54d86918a91" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df53af64-a7fb-5920-a9ae-4765f5d7236c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120029" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{100}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}}{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={50}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot {50}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡({100}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{100}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{50}^{∘}}{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}}}{2-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sin}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}}{2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing  the Pythagorean identity ${sin}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}=1-{cos}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} & =\\frac{1-{cos}^{2}⁡{50}^{∘}}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})}{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})}(1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘})}{\\cancel{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡{50}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386973 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147618 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1233be4b-7634-5882-9ae5-3200a9da71d8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Surface Area of a Polyhedron Given the Areas of the Faces: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "27a702ee-79d1-55c6-8b8d-dc8a7f0f77db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "336\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386975} {:answer/id #uuid "3e5df382-1586-5105-af93-061d2effa30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386976} {:answer/id #uuid "b73a068b-a624-57c6-8f23-a62443cd2759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "354\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386977} {:answer/id #uuid "13ab4dd1-997e-50aa-8938-fe73f4c8d489" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "348\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386978} {:answer/id #uuid "9f5375b8-a182-53a8-881e-1b45a8a8313f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "316\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386979}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e5df382-1586-5105-af93-061d2effa30e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "324\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386976} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f65bd7f-3d3f-5045-8487-612d7385d46c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386974}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2c2645ff-704d-5d0e-a816-127a2f8fd501" :question/math-academy-id "q-120078" :question/problem "![](images/8b/8b618c8e43dbe898a6e1c481f4f887a50bc2e8c658368876be11ec5ea269a50c.png)\n\nA company produces a chocolate bar that comes in an triangular-prism-shaped box. In the figure, the areas of some of the faces are displayed in a net of the prism. What is the total surface area of the box?" :question/worked-solution "The surface area of our prism consists of $2$ triangles (base and top) and $3$ rectangles (central and side faces). \n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Surface area} & =2\\times (\\text{Area of the base})+2\\times (\\text{Area of the side faces}) \\\\  & +1\\times (\\text{Area of the central face}) \\\\  & =2\\times 36+2\\times 72+108 \\\\  & =72+144+108 \\\\  & =324\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af31ad74-3d71-5216-82f4-241c0fe0d883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395993} {:answer/id #uuid "55344227-ef64-559d-b72e-d67580b900d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395994} {:answer/id #uuid "8c8d3009-744a-5594-85d0-81623acfa040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395995} {:answer/id #uuid "1acecbea-2330-5d66-8f7e-c711b7d73ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395996} {:answer/id #uuid "cabd1c0f-3241-5f02-a31b-66428bbc54cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af31ad74-3d71-5216-82f4-241c0fe0d883" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395993} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3af571d-ad32-5475-bbe0-4ca42f82e8b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186395992}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d2b3861b-a381-5b7c-bc56-9232209a631c" :question/math-academy-id "q-12008" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\nII. $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\nIII. $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}kx+ky \\\\ 4kx+3ky\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}x+y \\\\ 4x+3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{y}_{1}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{y}_{1} \\\\ 4{x}_{1}+3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2}+{y}_{2} \\\\ 4{x}_{2}+3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092220 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c4034e61-63dc-5f72-8275-e9c81290767d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polar Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6979975-c4fc-5dd6-a7c2-9f66b3cec100" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/9d/9d77180cde3a0c2ca1ad5187a6b64c6b7d0d282b0e673e24c50132d7e3d91ce3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386989} {:answer/id #uuid "3aebcf64-2103-5b69-9394-e1bc7f1d2130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/725d3670d3f2e50a9359e1573b3acf3d947b75f2318b8f84687a19d3f3182565.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386990} {:answer/id #uuid "00e21cf7-1e3e-5ee9-9e9d-d78979ba3a63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/0a/0a6fc2c09a48ff2b3ff05cee80fe2192b3c4df73349cb9aca0ca85bb63f4b7a3.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386991} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7a85e1-b3d8-533d-87ab-76a9deb58d4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/85/8542e401ead2affa416f1094b01012f6cb5b1e885abb73a08c0beb81805c5f95.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386992} {:answer/id #uuid "f4fdcf46-e742-5684-b33f-a377c0fd9066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/14/141ad1c2adb9ac4f098887a82beb041c087a717686f72aa4e2c632066775f740.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386993}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3aebcf64-2103-5b69-9394-e1bc7f1d2130" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/72/725d3670d3f2e50a9359e1573b3acf3d947b75f2318b8f84687a19d3f3182565.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386990} :answer-field/id #uuid "472a64e6-0ae4-5eb4-904b-1786b1d5396b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dc3ee50e-9f02-59b6-9819-5a8625491ef3" :question/math-academy-id "q-120161" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve $r=9?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $r=9$ is a circle of radius $9$ centered at the origin. Its graph is given below.\n\n![](images/72/725d3670d3f2e50a9359e1573b3acf3d947b75f2318b8f84687a19d3f3182565.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "93f33baf-c3b5-549a-8eb7-1ab370e9c62b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Intersection of a Polar Circle With an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "155caee8-0023-56b3-b370-2fd485d05fc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250738} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc099f5-307d-559c-9a46-d23e6c48c70c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251081} {:answer/id #uuid "487f3e5a-7c1a-5b15-8ae9-ef95b5f4ca2e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-9)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251159} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0229c3-2f5b-56be-ae65-467349c66a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252199} {:answer/id #uuid "f3293cbf-6c9f-5280-b328-d11aba972662" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb0229c3-2f5b-56be-ae65-467349c66a08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252199} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0ed6a2b-c5aa-54ac-b872-3f04189b642e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c538a2a9-f441-50c2-b2b2-9978dbea640e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120169" :question/problem "Find the Cartesian coordinates of the point where the polar curve $r=3$ intersects the negative $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "The polar curve $r=3$ is a circle of radius $3$ centered at the origin. It crosses the negative $x$-axis at the point $(-3,0),$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/19/19c6d04de928de62e63fb949cfcd770d130f8836a0bf0da06d90e7d3cb5915b2.png)\n\nTherefore, the point of intersection is $(-3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253662 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "47358248-2067-590e-80e1-5d20796434c5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Circle Given a Point on the Circumference"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "500cff87-8b2a-5870-88e7-a6c045cafa6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251210} {:answer/id #uuid "951f9a13-1357-5138-88ce-cc8d213adbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251833} {:answer/id #uuid "bc35ee41-4106-5339-93ca-9d0eee337eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252215} {:answer/id #uuid "d33fc7ff-85d0-5239-8273-1cd18c6bbb61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252424} {:answer/id #uuid "f94fef92-646e-50c7-857f-2df18669976e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186252736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "500cff87-8b2a-5870-88e7-a6c045cafa6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "r=\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251210} :answer-field/id #uuid "8cf0b0d5-ec7a-5bf5-8b89-71870acdb90a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b3691fe4-f7f9-5f4e-89c5-6571f374e002" :question/math-academy-id "q-120186" :question/problem "![](images/f8/f8b8c044d79ce592089365b3918dfae0afc52ef86a71c899abe823c828ab23b0.png)\n\nFind the polar equation of the circle centered at the origin that passes through the point $A(1,-1)$, as shown above." :question/worked-solution "A circle of radius $k$ centered at the origin has the polar equation $r=k.$\n\nWe can compute the radius $k$ of our circle using the Pythagorean theorem, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the circle is $r=\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186253666 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092351 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6b04084c-8e69-5f53-a95d-664aba54aec9" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates: Second or Third Quadrant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c6f4f87-1ccd-589c-90e1-8890c55c15eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186250932} {:answer/id #uuid "62a869bc-7ad8-5514-a78a-02dff1b40c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251359} {:answer/id #uuid "89286d84-c732-59b4-956d-104585bfdb9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251711} {:answer/id #uuid "94083696-43f4-54f2-b3f6-a3866a469f41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\frac{\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251825} {:answer/id #uuid "a5579b86-e0b3-519e-954e-6b1008604303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a5579b86-e0b3-519e-954e-6b1008604303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186251978} :answer-field/id #uuid "cd003bc0-32be-5a18-932b-9a7f1e93dea2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0eb4fa3e-52f2-52cc-a5b3-7f2b007d645a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120234" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-\\sqrt{2})^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the reference angle, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})| \\\\  & =|\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{2}})| \\\\  & =|-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1)| \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies in the second quadrant, we subtract the reference angle from $\\pi ,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are  $(2,\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235285 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "58a49d00-cc58-5228-af38-9369cf31d8c8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Scalar Factor When Direction Reverses"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3cdd0854-261d-5ed4-92ef-477febc75b21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234639} {:answer/id #uuid "3eab765d-2302-5205-b5fd-0ada1e027409" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234642} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5da092-7603-5a36-a455-4137459af1b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234713} {:answer/id #uuid "dde73660-f588-5765-aec3-a2559c655608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234894} {:answer/id #uuid "e5f2c8c3-d49b-55df-8a1c-5ef41ca2446f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5f2c8c3-d49b-55df-8a1c-5ef41ca2446f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234905} :answer-field/id #uuid "959370f5-d157-5592-9470-19e5d767b960" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235154}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "16c001cc-439d-56cc-834f-f6d6a747b8e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-120257" :question/problem "![](images/55/551316d1e36175ca302c5c472d4109a9bc9cadf32cc85d2a1751424a386070cc.png)\n\nConsider the picture above. Given that $c=\\lambda a-2b,$ find $\\lambda .$" :question/worked-solution "To find $\\lambda ,$ we want to match the expression\n\n$$\nc=\\lambda a-2b.\n$$\n\nWe are given that one of the components is $-2b.$ So, we first draw the vector $-2b$ from the origin. Then we complete the path to the point $C.$\n\n![](images/40/40a0137d6227212db9b7ef9f349029838f3f51398790ec5e1c304e12c3743a0f.png)\n\nLet $P$ be the endpoint of the vector $-2b.$ Then\n\n$$\nc=\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC}.\n$$\n\nIn $△OPC,$ we have:\n\n- $PC=\\frac{1}{2}|a|$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{PC}$ and $a$ have opposite directions\n\nHence, $\\overset{\\to }{PC}=-\\frac{1}{2}a.$\n\nAdditionally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c & =\\overset{\\to }{OP}+\\overset{\\to }{PC} \\\\  & =-2b-\\frac{1}{2}a.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $c=\\lambda a-2b=-\\frac{1}{2}a-2b.$\n\nSince $a∦b,$ the linear combination can only be written in one way. Equating the coefficients of $a,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256384 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092352 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49bf1f94-d992-511d-9016-d471f2ad4358" :knowledge-point/title "Converting from Cartesian Coordinates to Polar Coordinates for a Point on an Axis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "564dfea9-94e5-5b80-a36f-acd4fd995ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254429} {:answer/id #uuid "59863691-0352-5356-82d2-ab1834b6534d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254453} {:answer/id #uuid "6e48c846-1d99-5e8c-8b19-40423da09a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254648} {:answer/id #uuid "a4a8930c-8a49-51c4-b618-54b52edceb02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(9,\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255123} {:answer/id #uuid "c876366e-402d-54a4-88fc-773f4c49db04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "564dfea9-94e5-5b80-a36f-acd4fd995ebb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254429} :answer-field/id #uuid "d14ab8bb-c93e-58c9-a40e-c0d3ca59cde8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256295}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f6623b3b-0ac5-59c1-a0a1-f8ed0a13b015" :question/math-academy-id "q-120295" :question/problem "A point $P$ has Cartesian coordinates $(3,0).$ What are the polar coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we determine $r:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $P$ lies on the positive half of the $x$-axis, we have $\\theta =0.$ \n\nTherefore, the polar coordinates of $P$ are $(3,0).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256387 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7f65d032-9acc-5fe6-b7c2-b8293576cb4c" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given in Polar Coordinates"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2075c86f-b918-575b-95e8-97859e70f90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253951} {:answer/id #uuid "52496fb6-06f9-54e1-953b-c03cfcd9334c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254387} {:answer/id #uuid "6724825c-031e-5303-abbe-a1eba30f6cba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254603} {:answer/id #uuid "8470b6c3-3213-5f69-8af6-c90604195120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254848} {:answer/id #uuid "b04df24b-bdd0-5a86-9ea8-ddd583bd9c28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{2},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52496fb6-06f9-54e1-953b-c03cfcd9334c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254387} :answer-field/id #uuid "5736a212-726e-5d37-950e-cb5984448244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256090}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cffd31fb-6e24-58d9-bedb-a4c483fcb431" :question/math-academy-id "q-120311" :question/problem "The point $P$ has polar coordinates $(4,{225}^{∘}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $P.$" :question/worked-solution "To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256393 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087669 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ec37c5e-8a40-58a7-8af3-4457cd5da43f" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05d4fe96-e437-5459-b90d-0a3bdfc1825d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253714} {:answer/id #uuid "60f62d48-049d-5a6d-a9e6-66749d676bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254537} {:answer/id #uuid "988c0b62-37ae-5848-98ed-b1e6c7c32632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{35}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255019} {:answer/id #uuid "a00792a6-ab66-5c63-99fe-0e99a6d1bfd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255080} {:answer/id #uuid "d0cd2ac8-79c2-55c9-8923-9989a38dea95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255532}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d0cd2ac8-79c2-55c9-8923-9989a38dea95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255532} :answer-field/id #uuid "88f8237f-b76a-5905-b3b4-ed1d154b073e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256175}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "cf1faff6-afe0-59ea-a58f-f8289c637840" :question/math-academy-id "q-120348" :question/problem "Express $\\sqrt{3}{cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-\\sqrt{3}{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{3}({cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x-{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={35}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {35}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3}({cos}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘}-{sin}^{2}⁡{35}^{∘})=\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{70}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087670 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "12ff17ae-9319-5498-a5e0-1d49ddbef50a" :knowledge-point/title "Using Alternative Forms of Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Expressions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "157cee2f-b39d-5759-8141-9311f65baea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{110}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253857} {:answer/id #uuid "b658d1bf-666b-53ab-86f3-7ef9d39e3d7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255284} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a267b4-b9d7-542f-b375-9cbb1aec3915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{110}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255571} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c24ae1-987c-52e7-81fe-5a16178072dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{55}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255782} {:answer/id #uuid "f3389054-48e3-5025-9361-e8574b467c1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{110}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255799}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "157cee2f-b39d-5759-8141-9311f65baea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{110}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253857} :answer-field/id #uuid "20eaadd3-fcd2-5ba3-9fe8-b461ce34013a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "de163629-b9d5-5fdd-bc23-30faf9d38fa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120362" :question/problem "Express $\\sqrt{2}-2\\sqrt{2}{sin}^{2}⁡{55}^{∘}$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "Our expression can be factored as follows:\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{2}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{55}^{∘})\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={55}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{55}^{∘} & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {55}^{∘})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡{110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{2}(1-2{sin}^{2}⁡{55}^{∘}) & =\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{110}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256401 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087671 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2902b205-4508-55c6-9a68-f57d3afc9eab" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values of Trigonometric Expressions Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0efb571c-483a-563e-90d9-cb87e72aef2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253800} {:answer/id #uuid "936a3292-3003-538b-ac5a-bdafbe5c67b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254964} {:answer/id #uuid "ac064fe8-638c-5851-b167-3a2d65119de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255189} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d28c64-588e-564f-b3a3-554152e0f434" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255678} {:answer/id #uuid "f5362cb5-e5e6-5977-80e9-1d87eb6ccfaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac064fe8-638c-5851-b167-3a2d65119de0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255189} :answer-field/id #uuid "3966172c-baa0-58e5-bb01-12250853b673" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9ed8218-8b72-5f11-b5f2-bf16df5e7136" :question/math-academy-id "q-120369" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n1-2{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={75}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-2{sin}^{2}⁡({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot {75}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡{150}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256407 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a155516-d839-587a-bed9-42b9a2e072c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253747} {:answer/id #uuid "1129da34-d7d1-5e9f-8843-f5403e6bf93a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253820} {:answer/id #uuid "12c049a4-b3f5-57ad-8839-06ff45915613" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253833} {:answer/id #uuid "4009cc1a-951f-501b-bdc2-6e69fb5a6357" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4\\sqrt{3},-\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254222} {:answer/id #uuid "a9834412-7636-54d2-8673-12769dbe9d79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6,-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255166}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a155516-d839-587a-bed9-42b9a2e072c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6\\sqrt{3},-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253747} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4d936aa-147e-55d6-93f2-fbed67c48196" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0e5e2dd-de81-57dc-8848-06ae6eac4cea" :question/math-academy-id "q-120455" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $({r}^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-3,\\sqrt{3}).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+(\\sqrt{3})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n((\\sqrt{12})^{2},\\frac{11\\pi }{6})=(12,\\frac{11\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =12(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(6\\sqrt{3},-6).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256409 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092219 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad049d53-af38-5917-b9d0-be965cadabcb" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Given Some Information About Another Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15a02ee7-0c84-56f1-bbf0-ebb90394da60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253859} {:answer/id #uuid "39bbabdf-ded0-54de-9bce-39f8a04d806d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254176} {:answer/id #uuid "6f9d34e5-3a45-5295-96d7-05130d5b7bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254659} {:answer/id #uuid "7a7d44b2-7e30-57fa-9135-30c7aad08a99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254758} {:answer/id #uuid "c869f985-2a3d-5a8b-ba06-6ea45dcdacab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255454}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39bbabdf-ded0-54de-9bce-39f8a04d806d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254176} :answer-field/id #uuid "8600f656-d47f-52c4-96f7-5d633c8eeb59" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256169}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6d3f790c-6d1a-5380-94bb-56056dda53c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-120468" :question/problem "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115181 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9a3d8db-a6e0-5c96-9c75-415d4a75cf4d" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equations With No Solutions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36bbe8cc-ddc2-5919-b270-7b19d0e48220" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400005} {:answer/id #uuid "0cb03412-8e94-5385-8192-91a634686b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400006} {:answer/id #uuid "00f5a9e7-5ff9-5cab-b4da-0f197eec9b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400007} {:answer/id #uuid "7e59753b-1300-571d-af93-523d0d3e5ed0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400008} {:answer/id #uuid "7632167a-b938-5b51-97c0-caab71755856" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00f5a9e7-5ff9-5cab-b4da-0f197eec9b0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400007} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b975aa1-8b82-5577-bfb9-2e2bcc3315d9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400004}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a9e6cde4-30d8-5716-a4a4-97a88908653f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120485" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}?$\n\nI. $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\nII. $\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x+2=2\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ $$\nIII. $7\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-5$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=7$ $$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{3}}$\n- $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=-\\frac{5}{7}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387015 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115182 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145bdcf2-b1d5-54a8-bd95-4b054d7a33f0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8af1639e-fabe-50f7-9169-1b4edc031f7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387017} {:answer/id #uuid "606b60d6-6925-52a7-9990-45232a53c94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387018} {:answer/id #uuid "a7eef4c8-eeed-522e-a869-4cc328c34fe0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387019} {:answer/id #uuid "b67293a3-a05b-5dd0-b551-121096a709c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "79\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387020} {:answer/id #uuid "d14149ef-09e5-57ac-afbe-9dbe9e2ee161" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387021}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "606b60d6-6925-52a7-9990-45232a53c94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "69\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387018} :answer-field/id #uuid "96f86185-7829-51c3-a1ea-36683535a8d5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "398ff4f2-a514-55ba-ad0a-235934ea5e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-120532" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3=-1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for ${0}^{∘}\\le x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation, isolating $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+3 & =-1-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ 2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-4 \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}⁡x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value: \n\n$$\nx=\\text{arcsin}(-\\frac{1}{2})=-{30}^{∘}\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add ${360}^{∘}.$ \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=x+{360}^{∘}=-{30}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={330}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo,  our first solution is ${x}_{1}={330}^{∘}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.$\n\n![](images/b3/b3c523e882baf0b3a105e10131091e40de686b4c1483f46e7bdc235e30743a6d.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions are $x={210}^{∘},{330}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=210\\cdot 330=69\\,300.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256417 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105727 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d766de83-9394-57be-84a4-bb88f5ddf792" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Magnitude of a Vector Given in Component Form"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4ad94684-b259-5c68-93b3-6c670af48230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254313} {:answer/id #uuid "51c9cf4e-46c9-5836-96a4-58587a21fa7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254379} {:answer/id #uuid "81b1e638-bbdb-5d08-96e3-a8f22fe6ada5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254818} {:answer/id #uuid "c146863e-e23b-597a-ac92-8a57382b02e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255392} {:answer/id #uuid "c1b70220-563a-503e-8ef2-47cc62c99cf4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51c9cf4e-46c9-5836-96a4-58587a21fa7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254379} :answer-field/id #uuid "f33f2913-d760-5769-a653-4234851581ce" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256352}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "10010191-d9e5-5c11-888d-317965c9e088" :question/math-academy-id "q-120543" :question/problem "Given that $w=⟨-1,0,3⟩$, find $|\\,w\\,|.$" :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\,w\\,| & =\\sqrt{{w}_{x}^{2}+{w}_{y}^{2}+{w}_{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+0+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256418 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096725 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef975f49-bb8f-5493-abff-225d8d5be778" :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Involving Mutually Exclusive Events: Word Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0936487e-5454-5687-83ed-78621369891c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253738} {:answer/id #uuid "271a98c1-94c5-5941-aecb-d70ee45a1503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254015} {:answer/id #uuid "571b3010-4986-52e5-9a8d-af6f49a1d619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254437} {:answer/id #uuid "6ee09344-02ed-5cb5-a0be-840c3cbd118a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254655} {:answer/id #uuid "8237f27f-12e1-578d-9a70-e5d888405c93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "271a98c1-94c5-5941-aecb-d70ee45a1503" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{19}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254015} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7e2bb51-d59c-5d36-9863-91fcad8a3f21" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256273}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ef44a98c-d5e4-53f7-ad1a-45b75b68cb54" :question/math-academy-id "q-120545" :question/problem "On a shelf, there are $11$ novels and $9$ science books. $1$ of the novels and $3$ of the science books are illustrated. The illustrated novel is taken from the shelf and set apart. If a single book is selected at random, what is the probability that it is a novel or an illustrated book?" :question/worked-solution "Let $I$ be the event that the selected book is an illustrated book, and let $N$ be the event that it is a novel. Then the probability that the selected book is a novel or an illustrated book is given by $P(I\\cup N).$\n\nSince the only illustrated novel was removed, there are no novels left on the shelf that are also illustrated. Therefore, $I$ and $N$ are mutually exclusive events, i.e., $P(I\\cap N)=0.$\n\nSince the illustrated novel was set apart, there are $10+9=19$ books remaining, of which $10$ are novels and $3$ are illustrated. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I) & =\\frac{3}{19}, \\\\ P(N) & =\\frac{10}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by the addition law, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(I\\cup N) & =P(I)+P(N)-P(I\\cap N) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{19}+\\frac{10}{19}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{3+10}{19} \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{19}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f9ccf6b5-e087-5f7b-88e3-3767efbdcc26" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Involving Cosecant with Non-Special Angles"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02ff9959-ca10-5d50-995e-9e7fc0a2ad71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21.6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253691} {:answer/id #uuid "1e6614a7-5303-5a86-b8cd-80177e3f6c89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18.3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253927} {:answer/id #uuid "cf956aba-0090-5814-94bf-4c8327470726" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255518} {:answer/id #uuid "de82359d-710c-5888-a2ae-b5acf0631f1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255645} {:answer/id #uuid "fc2b8802-7b55-5d8a-a56d-40851bbbb9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc2b8802-7b55-5d8a-a56d-40851bbbb9e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255886} :answer-field/id #uuid "dad99ebe-2b05-56c4-9d59-745b53b7613f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256311}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18ab8ec6-b553-56a9-a2fc-b97b7ef3fec1" :question/math-academy-id "q-120556" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-6$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi ,$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2},$ rounded to one decimal place?" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x},$ therefore the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{1}{6})=-0.167\\,448…≈-0.167\\,4\n$$\n\nThis value is outside the required domain. To get a value inside the range, we add $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-0.167\\,4+2\\pi =6.115\\,785…≈6.115\\,8\n$$\n\nOur first solution lies in the fourth quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x<0,$ the second solution must lie in the third quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=0.167\\,4.$\n\n![](images/f6/f6f08d799cd2d2e80494d94cc3b18a61f73227b88b25d79c926d65e161d4ce3a.png)\n\nSince the second solution ${x}_{2}$ lies in the $3$rd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +0.167\\,4 \\\\  & ≈3.309\\,0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=6.115\\,8\\cdot 3.309\\,0=20.2\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256425 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0484ad77-2f99-5c81-a6cb-a1158be44d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-2,-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253704} {:answer/id #uuid "15246ab6-3ecb-5633-9b4e-b73f8ed07f54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-4\\sqrt{2},-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253853} {:answer/id #uuid "31095a2e-0934-55af-abe5-a2f05014f85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254098} {:answer/id #uuid "3f307f37-58c4-56a4-a6dd-e9b99b686e71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,4\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254213} {:answer/id #uuid "e1629e74-354a-56e5-bbe5-bfd524f9a56f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,-2\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "31095a2e-0934-55af-abe5-a2f05014f85f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(4,-4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254098} :answer-field/id #uuid "e62cfd60-2bdd-5ae1-8632-0391624de18c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256330}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4cadc848-bed5-5538-a431-ac83515fffa4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120597" :question/problem "![](images/fd/fd38e4bed4f11f07f2c6901a74c7543655f5ff8a95b8b049e54ff4ff4dd409ee.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={165}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4\\sqrt{2}.$  \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${150}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={150}^{∘}+{165}^{∘}={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,-4).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256426 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092273 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "386a4f1a-4ee5-5bf7-aff2-e190c83f4529" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Cartesian Coordinates of a Point Using a Diagram"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9724a5f6-c9f4-536a-a17b-5425407b9c7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255002} {:answer/id #uuid "ea54590c-b0df-5003-9417-4c09af7236b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255723} {:answer/id #uuid "eb87a981-771e-5056-8d37-a93f3b1e2bef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255730} {:answer/id #uuid "feab8e4b-ee4c-5116-8ef8-f1f48cf2bd2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255909} {:answer/id #uuid "ff088a24-bea8-531d-8f92-d5dff5c8b72a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2,\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea54590c-b0df-5003-9417-4c09af7236b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255723} :answer-field/id #uuid "47c6223c-72f8-56bd-b93f-b161db175405" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256059}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1f77f386-bd94-517d-b58d-73799834bcb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-120598" :question/problem "![](images/47/475bda1ee758774ec9b07ec12a0ed92e56c4b571fd39f6e1ecca439278106ea6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(2,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={195}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" :question/worked-solution "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=2.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $2,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $2.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{195}^{∘}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(2,{45}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡({45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\sqrt{2},\\sqrt{2}).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387022 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "407616a9-e448-5652-9882-a1edf19600f8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Radial Line Given Its Polar Equation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eeb3ea5c-e17a-5133-8dd6-69250aaec174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/47/479df1b8aafdc4c061d221cbf5079958289178d982a33bb118934850ff889373.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387024} {:answer/id #uuid "ab5ee7f1-310c-57d0-a498-f20e66ba5c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f548b48d223ba5164e8e7335b67dc27dd89347bc368be0cc992c456fe5360b85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387025} {:answer/id #uuid "e3fa4b48-3218-5177-81c4-7d373df6d679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/82/82bf2fb643852163329b767d2cf8767c9ed8411060092f1652143e3842727ca2.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387026} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8981da-b767-535a-87cb-a0ffd8c2a5bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/79/7931fc318d080dbace3b1fea585f5d31449fc2dd4ceda000d659cacb43096beb.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387027} {:answer/id #uuid "0fe59ba9-dbba-5706-910e-8bda39e456c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/42/423d9cb637371b49b351145113d40905ea786029c244ac949ee5059ea5c7315b.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387028}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ab5ee7f1-310c-57d0-a498-f20e66ba5c3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045497 :db/ident :answer.type/image} :answer/value "images/f5/f548b48d223ba5164e8e7335b67dc27dd89347bc368be0cc992c456fe5360b85.png" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387025} :answer-field/id #uuid "c381b20e-76c5-566c-ad47-b7731e93267b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387023}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e4c78c8d-b767-562b-87b5-e9b873530467" :question/math-academy-id "q-120607" :question/problem "Which of the following is the graph of the polar curve  $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}?$" :question/worked-solution "The polar curve defined by the equation $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ is the ray that starts at the origin and makes a counterclockwise angle of $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ with the positive $x$-axis.\n\nThe correct answer is shown below.\n\n![](images/f5/f548b48d223ba5164e8e7335b67dc27dd89347bc368be0cc992c456fe5360b85.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186400010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3e99f652-e012-503c-97ae-9e43e478b1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400012} {:answer/id #uuid "c83a4309-acbf-5679-9e28-465cf4e9d799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400013} {:answer/id #uuid "6f833c32-c9f2-5bff-b68d-05c9a5598f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400014} {:answer/id #uuid "8444d1db-6a27-52c8-bd42-58d682c3d6b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400015} {:answer/id #uuid "68a0723d-e0c2-5c94-b776-962ebe8b99f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400016}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e99f652-e012-503c-97ae-9e43e478b1cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400012} :answer-field/id #uuid "70f71519-cda9-5a9b-a1db-b744f73d4f1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186400011}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93bbf63d-41f5-5cec-8b52-34cf770cf20e" :question/math-academy-id "q-120614" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nI. $5-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\nII. $\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$\nIII. $5\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-3=-2$" :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=1$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$\n- $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=\\frac{1}{5}$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equations II and III has no solutions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256434 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110665 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "91100afb-9a36-57c0-9f30-4692cfef0b93" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent and Other Identities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0563d615-ffa2-5a2e-a34a-325a3b3cd4b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253709} {:answer/id #uuid "4c3a13ca-0c91-59ed-aa40-aec07d5043d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254324} {:answer/id #uuid "79b3ea54-b3ba-5165-91dc-a1dbc40be7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254747} {:answer/id #uuid "7b13a578-e470-566f-813b-75474312fb7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254763} {:answer/id #uuid "a2fe877d-f681-58d7-ad42-7a9c39a14713" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{cot}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79b3ea54-b3ba-5165-91dc-a1dbc40be7c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cot}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254747} :answer-field/id #uuid "460c587f-198c-589f-9fc8-f74bfbf671fa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256051}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93375bd6-9a3b-584f-abb5-493a4e9eddf0" :question/math-academy-id "q-120631" :question/problem "$\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNext, we recall the following identities:\n\n$$\n1-{cos}^{2}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x,\\,\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x},\\,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}\n$$\n\n \n\nUsing the above identities, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\cdot ({sec}^{2}⁡x)} & =\\frac{1}{({sin}^{2}⁡x\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{{sin}^{2}⁡x}{{cos}^{2}⁡x})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{tan}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & ={cot}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)(1+{tan}^{2}⁡x)}={cot}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256437 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110666 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7640012b-79f9-5f6b-a26b-a7f4fe8c0e7b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Secant-Tangent Identity: Replacing a Squared Secant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14d2b769-b7bb-53fc-b947-c01c1ce161df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253851} {:answer/id #uuid "78a4b292-89ce-53d4-8475-fdda7bfe40c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254736} {:answer/id #uuid "a7224c22-fb60-5cd3-8f52-95f22c5e7f2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255146} {:answer/id #uuid "bd06b950-4b37-5d7d-b639-ef073390a2b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255361} {:answer/id #uuid "f8e3c92f-f95d-57db-a7ca-85394f5ddfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8e3c92f-f95d-57db-a7ca-85394f5ddfcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255850} :answer-field/id #uuid "7edb85ea-33a3-57f0-b0e9-29b6a66d467c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256159}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7483b5c0-3dc3-538f-b1c8-e2da92110f3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-120637" :question/problem "${tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta =$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the secant-tangent identity:\n\n$$\n1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe rewrite the given expression using the secant-tangent identity as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1+{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta =2{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186387036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115309 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6e126b20-0507-5f22-ba34-a55157ca7fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Trigonometric Equation When the Cotangent Function Equals Zero"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6712c057-d325-5087-b265-9e611b0283bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387038} {:answer/id #uuid "4452d378-3fea-57aa-ad62-dddc038b7c95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16\\,200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387039} {:answer/id #uuid "c49e8952-c40a-5925-9655-9513fef89265" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37\\,800" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387040} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e4311a-dd4f-5e64-911c-b5db5bfadeac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387041} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4ac064-11fb-5475-9f74-b2e7190d838c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18\\,900" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387042}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6712c057-d325-5087-b265-9e611b0283bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24\\,300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387038} :answer-field/id #uuid "b941e16d-5be1-50c8-953e-51bfad0efd5b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186387037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e429c53-db66-50b4-900e-8aac7266bdd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-120646" :question/problem "Given that ${x}_{1}$ and ${x}_{2}$ are the solutions to the equation $7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7$ for ${0}^{∘}<x<{360}^{∘},$ what is the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange the equation to isolate $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =3\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x+7 \\\\ -4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cot}⁡x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can't find an equivalent equation involving tangent, since this would lead to us dividing by $0:$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{1}{0}=\\text{undefined}\n$$\n\nHowever, we can instead find an equivalent equation using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}:$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=0\n$$\n\nIn this case, we can find the principal solution as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x & =0 \\\\ x & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡0 \\\\ x & ={90}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value lies inside the desired domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={90}^{∘}.$\n\nTo find more solutions, we add integer multiples of ${180}^{∘}$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={x}_{1}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={90}^{∘}+{180}^{∘} \\\\  & ={270}^{∘}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our solutions are $x={90}^{∘},{270}^{∘}.$\n\nFinally, the numerical value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$ is \n\n$$\n90\\cdot 270=24\\,300.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256442 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188221 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eda44360-52ec-59f7-98b4-f8d866a465e9" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression Using the Alternate Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "46c1a484-6908-532c-aead-33b816e61909" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254267} {:answer/id #uuid "90079a6b-99a3-58e1-ba8f-4b3406cda978" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254935} {:answer/id #uuid "a99148bc-1b88-5e2f-b08f-0fc60dd809e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255167} {:answer/id #uuid "b54f4930-c7c7-5a78-8cae-4edc271582ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255268} {:answer/id #uuid "e5bfeaa7-27c9-5375-a531-9b28a3d3f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5bfeaa7-27c9-5375-a531-9b28a3d3f21a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255697} :answer-field/id #uuid "12c1913c-48ce-5922-b686-38b55407977e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c59c3ea2-945d-5573-80ad-ccc1ae2f546f" :question/math-academy-id "q-120652" :question/problem "$\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡x={sec}^{2}⁡x-1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡x-{tan}^{2}⁡x=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the following identity: \n\n$$\n1-{sin}^{2}⁡x={cos}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nUsing the above identity, we can further simplify our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-(1-{sin}^{2}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x-1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}{\\cancel{{sec}^{2}⁡x}}-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-{cos}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x \\\\  & =1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡x}{{sec}^{2}⁡x}-1+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1-2{cos}^{2}⁡x.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256445 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "141d61c7-1d82-52fe-b977-19daa910da9b" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring Alternative Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ce8fd15-b91e-59d1-81c9-58484689b5d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253776} {:answer/id #uuid "0eba9484-8340-5e15-861e-9c9af85a9097" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253797} {:answer/id #uuid "426cdd7e-6f1c-501f-9e15-f8954d1d45c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254236} {:answer/id #uuid "4e0f5c03-c859-58db-8cab-b40af3b4dbd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254342} {:answer/id #uuid "e8ef8d91-6b04-56af-97d6-aafedb51fd25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "426cdd7e-6f1c-501f-9e15-f8954d1d45c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254236} :answer-field/id #uuid "83a7647a-f01a-56fa-ba8f-a9dac8ad5b07" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256168}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c1861048-306a-5c9d-bc3a-136c2abd3f1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120657" :question/problem "Simplify $\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}=\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression by factoring the numerator as a difference of squares, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1} & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1)(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1)\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}}{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1)}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +1}=\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta -1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256449 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188217 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9b14ef98-8122-512a-9cce-6e44392959d8" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying an Expression by Factoring and Using Various Forms of the Secant-Tangent Identity"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05207395-02f1-5d5f-9dc3-81acf6c01fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253706} {:answer/id #uuid "295e109a-6294-5fe6-9b3a-bf16636fb649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254040} {:answer/id #uuid "851af266-742f-54a9-a2c7-0b59d5c8ba4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254852} {:answer/id #uuid "93aa7902-3669-5423-ac92-e34b15e642b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254967} {:answer/id #uuid "fa687d80-88a9-583e-b3f7-707957e10df3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sec}⁡\\theta +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255866}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "851af266-742f-54a9-a2c7-0b59d5c8ba4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254852} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef30c5cd-87ee-54bb-9ffe-d545fe802caa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b8a55c6e-077f-5804-ac89-ec1be2eb0726" :question/math-academy-id "q-120666" :question/problem "Which of the following is equivalent to $\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the different forms of the secant-tangent Identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & (1) & 1+{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & (2) & {tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & (3) & {sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can rewrite our expression using $(2),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can further simplify our expression and use again $(2)$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } & =\\frac{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta ({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }({tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)}{\\cancel{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }} \\\\  & ={tan}^{2}⁡\\theta -1 \\\\  & =({sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -1)-1 \\\\  & ={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{{tan}^{4}⁡\\theta -{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta +1}{{tan}^{2}⁡\\theta }={sec}^{2}⁡\\theta -2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256452 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133752 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d90b9572-cdac-51f7-b386-81b750642585" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Polar Equation of a Radial Line That Passes Through a Given Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17f00b72-9daa-5a34-a8b3-6a2e5fa1dff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253878} {:answer/id #uuid "86521eb5-22cc-53a1-b524-b9ff1c2e311a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186254859} {:answer/id #uuid "9a84e848-6840-5e58-bc55-0334ef160334" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255033} {:answer/id #uuid "ad70fad3-8d19-5e8f-8a88-ee9f19172dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255199} {:answer/id #uuid "afd98217-57eb-5e73-ad53-c1c9d3a58fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255216}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad70fad3-8d19-5e8f-8a88-ee9f19172dd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255199} :answer-field/id #uuid "7360892e-3cc3-5430-8827-7517c29afa89" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6e7c36f9-b648-5bd3-b641-575b95a2bb92" :question/math-academy-id "q-120704" :question/problem "![](images/03/03814c00b9e29f8a05507616ed08e3fe3daa484336e461012dc6c80799efc9e0.png)\n\nGiven that the ray above passes through the point with Cartesian coordinates $(-\\sqrt{6},-\\sqrt{2})$, find the polar equation of the ray." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the reference angle associated with the point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-\\sqrt{2}}{-\\sqrt{6}}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the ray lies in the third quadrant. So, we find the angle in the third quadrant that has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the polar equation of the ray is \n\n$$\n\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}]}
